window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2020-12-2", "source": "2020年12月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part , you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on the topic Changes in the way of Transportation. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "How people travel has changed dramatically over the past few decades. In the past, most of my grandparents'\ngeneration walked or rode bicycles to work, and a trip to another city took a whole day by slow train.\nNowadays, the picture looks completely different.\nThe most obvious change lies in the variety and speed of transport. Today we can take the subway across a\nhuge city in minutes, reach a distant province by high-speed rail in a few hours, or simply open an app to call a\ncar or unlock a shared bike. Two forces drive this shift. Technological progress makes fast and smart travel\npossible, while rising incomes and growing environmental concerns push people toward high-speed rail and\nnew-energy vehicles.\nIn my view, these changes have made our lives far more convenient and our cities greener. As technology\nkeeps advancing, I firmly believe transportation will become even faster, cleaner and smarter in the years\nto come."}, "translation": {"prompt": "春节前夕吃团圆饭是中国人的传统。团圆饭是一年中最重要的晚餐，也是家庭团聚的最佳时机，家人生活在不同地方的家庭尤其如此。团圆饭上的菜肴丰富多样，其中有些菜肴有特殊含义。例如，鱼是不可缺少的一道菜，因为汉语中的\"鱼\"字和\"余\"字听上去一样。在中国的许多地方，饺子也是一道重要的佳肴，因为饺子象征着财富和好运。", "sample": "It is a tradition for Chinese people to have the reunion dinner on Chinese New Year's Eve. The reunion dinner is the most important dinner of the year and also the best occasion for family reunion, especially for families whose members live in different places. The dishes served at the reunion dinner are rich and varied, some of which have special meanings. For example, fish is an indispensable dish, because the Chinese character for \"fish\" (yu) sounds the same as the character for \"surplus\" (yu). In many parts of China, dumplings are also an important dish, because they symbolize wealth and good luck."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2020-12-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "When my son completes a task, I can't help but praise him. It's only natural to give praise where praise is due, right? But is there such a thing as too much praise?\nAccording to psychologist Katherine Phillip, children don't benefit from  __26__  praise as much as we'd like to think. \"Parents often praise, believing they are building their child's self-confidence. However, over-praising can have a  __27__  effect,\" says Phillip. \"When we use the same praise  __28__  , it may become empty and no longer valued by the child. It can also become an expectation that anything they do must be  __29__  with praise. This may lead to the child avoiding taking risks due to fear of  __30__  their parents.\"\nDoes this mean we should do away with all the praise? Phillip says no. \"The key to healthy praise is to focus on the process rather than the  __31__  . It is the recognition of a child's attempt, or the process in which they achieved something, that is essential,\" she says. \"Parents should encourage their child to take the risks needed to learn and grow.\"\nSo how do we break the  __32__  of praise we're all so accustomed to? Phillip says it's important to  __33__  between \"person praise\" and \"process praise\". \"Person praise is  __34__  saying how great someone is. It's a form of personal approval. Process praise is acknowledgement of the efforts the person has just  __35__  . Children who receive person praise are more likely to feel shame after losing,\" says Phillip.", "matchIntro": "Poverty Is a Story About Us, Not Them", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Too often still, we think we know what poverty looks like. It's the way we've been taught, the images we've been force-fed for decades. The chronically homeless. The undocumented immigrant. The urban poor, usually personified as a woman of color, the \"welfare queen\" politicians still too often reference."}, {"label": "B", "text": "But as income inequality rises to record levels in the United States, even in the midst of a record economic expansion, those familiar images are outdated, hurtful, and counterproductive to focusing attention on solutions and building ladders of opportunity."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Today's faces of income inequality and lack of opportunity look like all of us. It's Anna Landre, a disabled Georgetown University student fighting to keep health benefits that allow her the freedom to live her life. It's Tiffanie Standard, a counselor for young women of color in Philadelphia who want to be tech entrepreneurs—but who must work multiple jobs to stay afloat. It's Ken Outlaw, a welder in rural North Carolina whose dream of going back to school at a local community college was dashed by Hurricane Florence—just one of the extreme weather events that have tipped the balance for struggling Americans across the nation."}, {"label": "D", "text": "If these are the central characters of our story about poverty, what layers of perceptions, myths, and realities must we unearth to find meaningful solutions and support? In pursuit of revealing this complicated reality, Mothering Justice, led by women of color, went last year to the state capital in Lansing, Michigan, to lobby on issues that affect working mothers. One of the Mothering Justice organizers went to the office of a state representative to talk about the lack of affordable childcare—the vestiges(痕迹) of a system that expected mothers to stay home with their children while their husbands worked. A legislative staffer dismissed the activist's concerns, telling her \"my husband took care of that—I stayed home.\""}, {"label": "E", "text": "That comment, says Mothering Justice director Danielle Atkinson, \"was meant to shame\" and relied on the familiar notion that a woman of color concerned about income inequality and programs that promote mobility must by definition be a single mom, probably with multiple kids. In this case, the Mothering Justice activist happened to be married. And in most cases in the America of 2019, the images that come to mind when we hear the words poverty or income inequality fail miserably in reflecting a complicated reality: poverty touches virtually all of us. The face of income inequality, for all but a very few of us, is the one we each see in the mirror."}, {"label": "F", "text": "How many of us are poor in the U.S.? It depends on who you ask. According to the Census Bureau, 38 million people in the U.S. are living below the official poverty thresholds. Taking into account economic need beyond that absolute measure, the Institute for Policy Studies found that 140 million people are poor or low-income. That's almost half the U.S. population."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Whatever the measure, within that massive group, poverty is extremely diverse. We know that some people are more affected than others, like children, the elderly, people with disabilities, and people of color."}, {"label": "H", "text": "But the fact that 4 in 10 Americans can't come up with $400 in an emergency is a commonly cited statistic for good reason: economic instability stretches across race, gender, and geography. It even reaches into the middle classes, as real wages have stagnated(不增长) for all but the very wealthy and temporary spells of financial instability are not uncommon."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Negative images remain of who is living in poverty as well as what is needed to move out of it. The big American myth is that you can pull yourself up by your own efforts and change a bad situation into a good one. The reality is that finding opportunity without help from families, friends, schools, and community is virtually impossible. And the playing field is nothing close to level."}, {"label": "J", "text": "The FrameWorks Institute, a research group that focuses on public framing of issues, has studied what sustains stereotypes and narratives of poverty in the United Kingdom. \"People view economic success and wellbeing in life as a product of choice, willpower, and drive,\" says Nat Kendall-Taylor, CEO of FrameWorks. \"When we see people who are struggling,\" he says, those assumptions \"lead us to the perception that people in poverty are lazy, they don't care, and they haven't made the right decisions.\""}, {"label": "K", "text": "Does this sound familiar? Similar ideas surround poverty in the U.S. And these assumptions give a false picture of reality. \"When people enter into that pattern of thinking,\" says Kendall-Taylor, \"it's cognitively comfortable to make sense of issues of poverty in that way. It creates a kind of cognitive blindness—all of the factors external to a person's drive and choices that they've made become invisible and fade from view.\""}, {"label": "L", "text": "Those external factors include the difficulties accompanying low-wage work or structural discrimination based on race, gender, or ability. Assumptions get worse when people who are poor use government benefits to help them survive. There is a great tension between \"the poor\" and those who are receiving what has become a dirty word: \"welfare.\""}, {"label": "M", "text": "According to the General Social Survey, 71 percent of respondents believe the country is spending too little on \"assistance to the poor\". On the other hand, 22 percent think we are spending too little on \"welfare\": 37 percent believe we are spending too much."}, {"label": "N", "text": "\"Poverty has been interchangeable with people of color—specifically black women and black mothers,\" says Atkinson of Mothering Justice. It's true that black mothers are more affected by poverty than many other groups, yet they are disproportionately the face of poverty. For example, Americans routinely overestimate the share of black recipients of public assistance programs."}, {"label": "O", "text": "In reality, most people will experience some form of financial hardship at some point in their lives. Indeed, people tend to dip in and out of poverty, perhaps due to unexpected obstacles like losing a job, or when hours of a low-wage job fluctuate."}, {"label": "P", "text": "Something each of us can do is to treat each other with the dignity and sympathy that is deserved and to understand deeply that the issue of poverty touches all of us."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Boredom has, paradoxically, become quite interesting to academics lately. In early May, London's Boring Conference celebrated seven years of delighting in dullness. At this event, people flocked to talks about weather, traffic jams and vending-machine sounds, among other sleep-inducing topics.\nWhat, exactly, is everybody studying? One widely accepted psychological definition of boredom is \"the distasteful experience of wanting, but being unable, to engage in satisfying activity.\" But how can you quantify a person's boredom level and compare it with someone else's? In 1986, psychologists introduced the Boredom Proneness Scale, designed to measure an individual's overall tendency to feel bored. By contrast, the Multidimensional State Boredom Scale, developed in 2008, measures a person's feelings of boredom in a given situation.\nBoredom has been linked to behavior issues including inattentive driving, mindless snacking, excessive drinking, and addictive gambling. In fact, many of us would choose pain over boredom. One team of psychologists discovered that two-thirds of men and a quarter of women would rather self-administer electric shocks than sit alone with their thoughts for 15 minutes. Researching this phenomenon, another team asked volunteers to watch boring, sad, or neutral films, during which they could self-administer electric shocks. The bored volunteers shocked themselves more and harder than the sad or neutral ones did.\nBut boredom isn't all bad. By encouraging self-reflection and daydreaming, it can spur creativity. An early study gave participants abundant time to complete problem-solving and word-association exercises. Once all the obvious answers were exhausted, participants gave more and more inventive answers to combat boredom. A British study took these findings one step further, asking subjects to complete a creative challenge (coming up with a list of alternative uses for a household item). One group of subjects did a boring activity first, while the others went straight to the creative task. Those whose boredom pumps had been primed were more productive.\nIn our always-connected world, boredom may be a hard-to-define state, but it is a fertile one. Watch paint dry or water boil, or at least put away your smartphone for a while, and you might unlock your next big idea.", "47": "Boredom has, paradoxically, become quite interesting to academics lately. In early May, London's Boring Conference celebrated seven years of delighting in dullness. At this event, people flocked to talks about weather, traffic jams and vending-machine sounds, among other sleep-inducing topics.\nWhat, exactly, is everybody studying? One widely accepted psychological definition of boredom is \"the distasteful experience of wanting, but being unable, to engage in satisfying activity.\" But how can you quantify a person's boredom level and compare it with someone else's? In 1986, psychologists introduced the Boredom Proneness Scale, designed to measure an individual's overall tendency to feel bored. By contrast, the Multidimensional State Boredom Scale, developed in 2008, measures a person's feelings of boredom in a given situation.\nBoredom has been linked to behavior issues including inattentive driving, mindless snacking, excessive drinking, and addictive gambling. In fact, many of us would choose pain over boredom. One team of psychologists discovered that two-thirds of men and a quarter of women would rather self-administer electric shocks than sit alone with their thoughts for 15 minutes. Researching this phenomenon, another team asked volunteers to watch boring, sad, or neutral films, during which they could self-administer electric shocks. The bored volunteers shocked themselves more and harder than the sad or neutral ones did.\nBut boredom isn't all bad. By encouraging self-reflection and daydreaming, it can spur creativity. An early study gave participants abundant time to complete problem-solving and word-association exercises. Once all the obvious answers were exhausted, participants gave more and more inventive answers to combat boredom. A British study took these findings one step further, asking subjects to complete a creative challenge (coming up with a list of alternative uses for a household item). One group of subjects did a boring activity first, while the others went straight to the creative task. Those whose boredom pumps had been primed were more productive.\nIn our always-connected world, boredom may be a hard-to-define state, but it is a fertile one. Watch paint dry or water boil, or at least put away your smartphone for a while, and you might unlock your next big idea.", "48": "Boredom has, paradoxically, become quite interesting to academics lately. In early May, London's Boring Conference celebrated seven years of delighting in dullness. At this event, people flocked to talks about weather, traffic jams and vending-machine sounds, among other sleep-inducing topics.\nWhat, exactly, is everybody studying? One widely accepted psychological definition of boredom is \"the distasteful experience of wanting, but being unable, to engage in satisfying activity.\" But how can you quantify a person's boredom level and compare it with someone else's? In 1986, psychologists introduced the Boredom Proneness Scale, designed to measure an individual's overall tendency to feel bored. By contrast, the Multidimensional State Boredom Scale, developed in 2008, measures a person's feelings of boredom in a given situation.\nBoredom has been linked to behavior issues including inattentive driving, mindless snacking, excessive drinking, and addictive gambling. In fact, many of us would choose pain over boredom. One team of psychologists discovered that two-thirds of men and a quarter of women would rather self-administer electric shocks than sit alone with their thoughts for 15 minutes. Researching this phenomenon, another team asked volunteers to watch boring, sad, or neutral films, during which they could self-administer electric shocks. The bored volunteers shocked themselves more and harder than the sad or neutral ones did.\nBut boredom isn't all bad. By encouraging self-reflection and daydreaming, it can spur creativity. An early study gave participants abundant time to complete problem-solving and word-association exercises. Once all the obvious answers were exhausted, participants gave more and more inventive answers to combat boredom. A British study took these findings one step further, asking subjects to complete a creative challenge (coming up with a list of alternative uses for a household item). One group of subjects did a boring activity first, while the others went straight to the creative task. Those whose boredom pumps had been primed were more productive.\nIn our always-connected world, boredom may be a hard-to-define state, but it is a fertile one. Watch paint dry or water boil, or at least put away your smartphone for a while, and you might unlock your next big idea.", "49": "Boredom has, paradoxically, become quite interesting to academics lately. In early May, London's Boring Conference celebrated seven years of delighting in dullness. At this event, people flocked to talks about weather, traffic jams and vending-machine sounds, among other sleep-inducing topics.\nWhat, exactly, is everybody studying? One widely accepted psychological definition of boredom is \"the distasteful experience of wanting, but being unable, to engage in satisfying activity.\" But how can you quantify a person's boredom level and compare it with someone else's? In 1986, psychologists introduced the Boredom Proneness Scale, designed to measure an individual's overall tendency to feel bored. By contrast, the Multidimensional State Boredom Scale, developed in 2008, measures a person's feelings of boredom in a given situation.\nBoredom has been linked to behavior issues including inattentive driving, mindless snacking, excessive drinking, and addictive gambling. In fact, many of us would choose pain over boredom. One team of psychologists discovered that two-thirds of men and a quarter of women would rather self-administer electric shocks than sit alone with their thoughts for 15 minutes. Researching this phenomenon, another team asked volunteers to watch boring, sad, or neutral films, during which they could self-administer electric shocks. The bored volunteers shocked themselves more and harder than the sad or neutral ones did.\nBut boredom isn't all bad. By encouraging self-reflection and daydreaming, it can spur creativity. An early study gave participants abundant time to complete problem-solving and word-association exercises. Once all the obvious answers were exhausted, participants gave more and more inventive answers to combat boredom. A British study took these findings one step further, asking subjects to complete a creative challenge (coming up with a list of alternative uses for a household item). One group of subjects did a boring activity first, while the others went straight to the creative task. Those whose boredom pumps had been primed were more productive.\nIn our always-connected world, boredom may be a hard-to-define state, but it is a fertile one. Watch paint dry or water boil, or at least put away your smartphone for a while, and you might unlock your next big idea.", "50": "Boredom has, paradoxically, become quite interesting to academics lately. In early May, London's Boring Conference celebrated seven years of delighting in dullness. At this event, people flocked to talks about weather, traffic jams and vending-machine sounds, among other sleep-inducing topics.\nWhat, exactly, is everybody studying? One widely accepted psychological definition of boredom is \"the distasteful experience of wanting, but being unable, to engage in satisfying activity.\" But how can you quantify a person's boredom level and compare it with someone else's? In 1986, psychologists introduced the Boredom Proneness Scale, designed to measure an individual's overall tendency to feel bored. By contrast, the Multidimensional State Boredom Scale, developed in 2008, measures a person's feelings of boredom in a given situation.\nBoredom has been linked to behavior issues including inattentive driving, mindless snacking, excessive drinking, and addictive gambling. In fact, many of us would choose pain over boredom. One team of psychologists discovered that two-thirds of men and a quarter of women would rather self-administer electric shocks than sit alone with their thoughts for 15 minutes. Researching this phenomenon, another team asked volunteers to watch boring, sad, or neutral films, during which they could self-administer electric shocks. The bored volunteers shocked themselves more and harder than the sad or neutral ones did.\nBut boredom isn't all bad. By encouraging self-reflection and daydreaming, it can spur creativity. An early study gave participants abundant time to complete problem-solving and word-association exercises. Once all the obvious answers were exhausted, participants gave more and more inventive answers to combat boredom. A British study took these findings one step further, asking subjects to complete a creative challenge (coming up with a list of alternative uses for a household item). One group of subjects did a boring activity first, while the others went straight to the creative task. Those whose boredom pumps had been primed were more productive.\nIn our always-connected world, boredom may be a hard-to-define state, but it is a fertile one. Watch paint dry or water boil, or at least put away your smartphone for a while, and you might unlock your next big idea.", "51": "Forests in countries like Brazil and the Congo get a lot of attention from environmentalists, and it is easy to see why. South America and sub-Saharan Africa are experiencing deforestation on an enormous scale: every year almost 5 million hectares are lost. But forests are also changing in rich Western countries. They are growing larger, both in the sense that they occupy more land and that the trees in them are bigger. What is going on?\nForests are spreading in almost all Western countries, with the fastest growth in places that historically had rather few trees. In 1990 28% of Spain was forested; now the proportion is 37%. In both Greece and Italy, the growth was from 26% to 32% over the same period. Forests are gradually taking more land in America and Australia. Perhaps most astonishing is the trend in Ireland. Roughly 1% of that country was forested when it became independent in 1922. Now forests cover 11% of the land, and the government wants to push the proportion to 18% by the 2040s.\nTwo things are fertilising this growth. The first is the abandonment of farmland, especially in high, dry places where nothing grows terribly well. When farmers give up trying to earn a living from farming or herding, trees simply move in. The second is government policy and subsidy. Throughout history, governments have protected and promoted forests for diverse reasons, ranging from the need for wooden warships to a desire to promote suburban house-building. Nowadays forests are increasingly welcome because they suck in carbon pollution from the air. The justifications change, the desire for more trees remains constant.\nThe greening of the West does not delight everyone. Farmers complain that land is being taken out of use by generously subsidised tree plantations. Parts of Spain and Portugal suffer from terrible forest fires. Others simply dislike the appearance of forests planted in neat rows. They will have to get used to the trees, however. The growth of Western forests seems almost as unstoppable as deforestation elsewhere.", "52": "Forests in countries like Brazil and the Congo get a lot of attention from environmentalists, and it is easy to see why. South America and sub-Saharan Africa are experiencing deforestation on an enormous scale: every year almost 5 million hectares are lost. But forests are also changing in rich Western countries. They are growing larger, both in the sense that they occupy more land and that the trees in them are bigger. What is going on?\nForests are spreading in almost all Western countries, with the fastest growth in places that historically had rather few trees. In 1990 28% of Spain was forested; now the proportion is 37%. In both Greece and Italy, the growth was from 26% to 32% over the same period. Forests are gradually taking more land in America and Australia. Perhaps most astonishing is the trend in Ireland. Roughly 1% of that country was forested when it became independent in 1922. Now forests cover 11% of the land, and the government wants to push the proportion to 18% by the 2040s.\nTwo things are fertilising this growth. The first is the abandonment of farmland, especially in high, dry places where nothing grows terribly well. When farmers give up trying to earn a living from farming or herding, trees simply move in. The second is government policy and subsidy. Throughout history, governments have protected and promoted forests for diverse reasons, ranging from the need for wooden warships to a desire to promote suburban house-building. Nowadays forests are increasingly welcome because they suck in carbon pollution from the air. The justifications change, the desire for more trees remains constant.\nThe greening of the West does not delight everyone. Farmers complain that land is being taken out of use by generously subsidised tree plantations. Parts of Spain and Portugal suffer from terrible forest fires. Others simply dislike the appearance of forests planted in neat rows. They will have to get used to the trees, however. The growth of Western forests seems almost as unstoppable as deforestation elsewhere.", "53": "Forests in countries like Brazil and the Congo get a lot of attention from environmentalists, and it is easy to see why. South America and sub-Saharan Africa are experiencing deforestation on an enormous scale: every year almost 5 million hectares are lost. But forests are also changing in rich Western countries. They are growing larger, both in the sense that they occupy more land and that the trees in them are bigger. What is going on?\nForests are spreading in almost all Western countries, with the fastest growth in places that historically had rather few trees. In 1990 28% of Spain was forested; now the proportion is 37%. In both Greece and Italy, the growth was from 26% to 32% over the same period. Forests are gradually taking more land in America and Australia. Perhaps most astonishing is the trend in Ireland. Roughly 1% of that country was forested when it became independent in 1922. Now forests cover 11% of the land, and the government wants to push the proportion to 18% by the 2040s.\nTwo things are fertilising this growth. The first is the abandonment of farmland, especially in high, dry places where nothing grows terribly well. When farmers give up trying to earn a living from farming or herding, trees simply move in. The second is government policy and subsidy. Throughout history, governments have protected and promoted forests for diverse reasons, ranging from the need for wooden warships to a desire to promote suburban house-building. Nowadays forests are increasingly welcome because they suck in carbon pollution from the air. The justifications change, the desire for more trees remains constant.\nThe greening of the West does not delight everyone. Farmers complain that land is being taken out of use by generously subsidised tree plantations. Parts of Spain and Portugal suffer from terrible forest fires. Others simply dislike the appearance of forests planted in neat rows. They will have to get used to the trees, however. The growth of Western forests seems almost as unstoppable as deforestation elsewhere.", "54": "Forests in countries like Brazil and the Congo get a lot of attention from environmentalists, and it is easy to see why. South America and sub-Saharan Africa are experiencing deforestation on an enormous scale: every year almost 5 million hectares are lost. But forests are also changing in rich Western countries. They are growing larger, both in the sense that they occupy more land and that the trees in them are bigger. What is going on?\nForests are spreading in almost all Western countries, with the fastest growth in places that historically had rather few trees. In 1990 28% of Spain was forested; now the proportion is 37%. In both Greece and Italy, the growth was from 26% to 32% over the same period. Forests are gradually taking more land in America and Australia. Perhaps most astonishing is the trend in Ireland. Roughly 1% of that country was forested when it became independent in 1922. Now forests cover 11% of the land, and the government wants to push the proportion to 18% by the 2040s.\nTwo things are fertilising this growth. The first is the abandonment of farmland, especially in high, dry places where nothing grows terribly well. When farmers give up trying to earn a living from farming or herding, trees simply move in. The second is government policy and subsidy. Throughout history, governments have protected and promoted forests for diverse reasons, ranging from the need for wooden warships to a desire to promote suburban house-building. Nowadays forests are increasingly welcome because they suck in carbon pollution from the air. The justifications change, the desire for more trees remains constant.\nThe greening of the West does not delight everyone. Farmers complain that land is being taken out of use by generously subsidised tree plantations. Parts of Spain and Portugal suffer from terrible forest fires. Others simply dislike the appearance of forests planted in neat rows. They will have to get used to the trees, however. The growth of Western forests seems almost as unstoppable as deforestation elsewhere.", "55": "Forests in countries like Brazil and the Congo get a lot of attention from environmentalists, and it is easy to see why. South America and sub-Saharan Africa are experiencing deforestation on an enormous scale: every year almost 5 million hectares are lost. But forests are also changing in rich Western countries. They are growing larger, both in the sense that they occupy more land and that the trees in them are bigger. What is going on?\nForests are spreading in almost all Western countries, with the fastest growth in places that historically had rather few trees. In 1990 28% of Spain was forested; now the proportion is 37%. In both Greece and Italy, the growth was from 26% to 32% over the same period. Forests are gradually taking more land in America and Australia. Perhaps most astonishing is the trend in Ireland. Roughly 1% of that country was forested when it became independent in 1922. Now forests cover 11% of the land, and the government wants to push the proportion to 18% by the 2040s.\nTwo things are fertilising this growth. The first is the abandonment of farmland, especially in high, dry places where nothing grows terribly well. When farmers give up trying to earn a living from farming or herding, trees simply move in. The second is government policy and subsidy. Throughout history, governments have protected and promoted forests for diverse reasons, ranging from the need for wooden warships to a desire to promote suburban house-building. Nowadays forests are increasingly welcome because they suck in carbon pollution from the air. The justifications change, the desire for more trees remains constant.\nThe greening of the West does not delight everyone. Farmers complain that land is being taken out of use by generously subsidised tree plantations. Parts of Spain and Portugal suffer from terrible forest fires. Others simply dislike the appearance of forests planted in neat rows. They will have to get used to the trees, however. The growth of Western forests seems almost as unstoppable as deforestation elsewhere."}, "questions": [{"id": "2020-12-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What does the news report say about the waiter?", "options": ["A) He wanted to buy a home.", "B) He suffered from a shock.", "C) He lost a huge sum of money.", "D) He did an unusual good deed."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He did an unusual good deed.\n【定位】A waiter has returned a check worth nearly $424,000 to the retired social worker who lost it.\n... He opened the envelope and got a shock. ... She tried to give him money later on, but he\ngraciously declined.\n一名服务员把一张面值近 424,000 美元、由一位退休社工弄丢的支票归还了。……他打开信\n封，吃了一惊。……她后来想给他钱，但他礼貌地谢绝了。\n【信号】开篇首句即点核心事件。新闻短消息的答案信号是\"开篇第一句的核心事件\"：A waiter has\nreturned a check worth nearly $424,000 to the retired social worker who lost it 一上\n来就交代了服务员做的事——归还巨额支票；再加上结尾 he graciously declined（婉拒酬\n谢），整段都在讲\"服务员归还失物、不图回报\"这件不寻常的好事。\n【替换】A waiter has returned a check worth nearly $424,000 ↔ did an unusual good deed\n具体行为概括为\"做好事\"\nhe graciously declined ↔ unusual\n婉拒酬谢印证\"不寻常\"\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音确实念到\"买房\"相关内容——钱是 already planned for a down payment on a new\nhome（已计划用作新房首付），但想买房的是丢钱的女顾客，不是服务员。本题问的是\nthe waiter，A 把顾客的打算安到服务员头上。\n借用原文：already planned for a down payment on a new home\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音里 shock 这个词真的出现了——He opened the envelope and got a shock（他打开\n信封吃了一惊），所以\"听到 shock 就选 B\"很诱人。但\"吃一惊\"只是他打开信封那一刻的瞬\n时反应，不是新闻要讲的他做的事，更不是 suffered from a shock（遭受打击/惊吓）这种\n持续受害的意思，答非所问。\n借用原文：He opened the envelope and got a shock\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音开篇就说支票是 the retired social worker who lost it（弄丢支票的退休社工）丢的，\n丢一大笔钱的是顾客，服务员恰恰是\"捡到并归还\"的人。C 把\"丢钱\"这一与服务员相反的角\n色硬安到服务员身上，与原文相反。\n借用原文：the retired social worker who lost it", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What did the customer try to do when she got her check back?", "options": ["A) Invite the waiter to a fancy dinner.", "B) Tell her story to the Daily News.", "C) Give some money to the waiter.", "D) Pay the waiter's school tuition."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Give some money to the waiter.\n【定位】She tried to give him money later on, but he graciously declined.\n她后来想给他钱，但他礼貌地谢绝了。\n【信号】题干 try 与原文 tried 直接对应。答案信号是题干关键词 try 在录音里的对应落点：She\ntried to give him money later on 中的 tried to give 与题干 try to do 严丝合缝，这一句\n就是承载\"她试图做什么\"的答案句；后半 but he graciously declined 说明只是\"试图\"、并\n未成功，正扣题干的 try。\n【替换】She tried to give him money later on ↔ Give some money to the waiter\ngive money 几乎原义对应\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音通篇只说她\"试图给他钱\"，从未提到 invite to a fancy dinner（请吃豪华大餐）这件\n事。A 是凭\"答谢恩人请吃饭\"的常识想当然，录音里查无此事。\nB) 张冠李戴\n录音里 Daily News 确实出现——the restaurant's owner called the Daily News for help\n（店主打电话向《每日新闻》求助），但联系报社的是餐厅店主，目的是找失主，不是顾客\n去\"讲述她的故事\"。B 盗用 Daily News 这个真出现的专名，把店主的行为安到顾客头上。\n借用原文：the restaurant's owner called the Daily News for help\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音结尾提到服务员 who's working his way through school（半工半读、靠打工供自己\n上学），\"school\"这个词真出现了，于是\"替他付学费\"很有迷惑性。但录音只说他自己边打\n工边读书，从没说顾客要替他付 school tuition（学费）；顾客试图做的只是泛泛\"给钱\"，\n并非专门付学费。\n借用原文：who's working his way through school\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 The village of Maref in Alaska voted on Tuesday to move to the state's mainland. The move is due to\nglobal warming and rising sea levels. Most of the village's 169 registered voters took part in the town\nhall meeting. They decided in a vote of 89 to 78 to move from their land on Sarichef Island, near the\nArctic Circle. Maref Council Secretary Donna Barr said the vote was largely symbolic. It will be costly\nfinancially to the community. \"About 15 years ago, they estimated the cost at $180 million. I would\nfigure it's much higher now,\" Barr said. \"We don't see the move happening in our lifetime because of the\nfunding.\" The village's roughly 650 residents have seen warming temperatures melt sea ice and\npermanently frozen land. This has resulted in houses falling into the water. At least 31 villages in Alaska\nface \"immediate threats\" due to climate change, the Government Accountability Office reported in 2009.\nAlaska的Maref村在周二投票决定迁往该州大陆。此次搬迁是由于全球变暖和海平面上升。该村169名登\n记选民中的大多数参加了市政厅会议。他们以89票对78票的结果决定从北极圈附近Sarichef岛上的土地搬\n迁。Maref委员会秘书Donna Barr表示，这次投票在很大程度上具有象征意义。这将给社区带来巨大的财\n政负担。\"大约15年前,他们估计费用为1.8亿美元。我认为现在肯定要高得多，\"Barr说。\"由于资金问题，\n我们认为这次搬迁不会在我们有生之年发生。\"该村约650名居民目睹了气温升高导致海冰融化和永久冻土\n消融。这导致房屋掉入水中。美国政府问责办公室在2009年报告称，Alaska至少有31个村庄因气候变化\n面临\"直接威胁\"。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What is Maref's vote on Tuesday about?", "options": ["A) Whether or not to move to the state's mainland.", "B) How to keep the village from sinking into the sea.", "C) Where to get the funds for rebuilding their village.", "D) What to do about the rising level of the seawater."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Whether or not to move to the state's mainland.\n【定位】The village of Maref in Alaska voted on Tuesday to move to the state's mainland.\n阿拉斯加的马雷夫村在周二投票决定搬迁到该州的大陆地区。\n【信号】开篇首句直陈事件。新闻报道的导语句往往把\"谁、何时、做了什么\"一次说清，本段开篇\nvoted on Tuesday to move to the state's mainland 就把投票时间与投票内容直接抛出，\n这种\"开篇即点题\"是新闻题最强的定位信号，不必等转折。\n【替换】voted on Tuesday to move to the state's mainland ↔\nWhether or not to move to the state's mainland.\n做了的事改写成投票议题\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音背景里确有\"海平面上升、房屋落水\"之意（houses falling into the water），B 用\nsinking into the sea 蹭这个画面，但投票要做的是\"搬走\"，不是\"想办法不让村子沉\"——\n把背景威胁错当成投票内容。\n借用原文：houses falling into the water\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音后段确实谈到钱（because of the funding），C 用 get the funds 蹭这个词，但\"资\n金\"是录音里解释\"为何搬不动\"的内容，不是这次投票要决定的事，属把后文的钱搬到投票议\n题上。\n借用原文：because of the funding\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音确说搬迁起因是 global warming and rising sea levels，D 用 rising level of the\nseawater 蹭这个原因，但那是\"为什么要搬\"的背景，不是\"投票决定做什么\"；把起因当成了\n投票内容。\n借用原文：global warming and rising sea levels", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "Why did Donna Barr say they wouldn't see the plan carried out any time soon?", "options": ["A) It takes too long a time.", "B) It costs too much money.", "C) It has to wait for the state's final approval.", "D) It faces strong opposition from many villagers."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It costs too much money.\n【定位】It will be costly financially to the community. \"We don't see the move happening in our\nlifetime because of the funding,\" Barr said.\n这对社区来说在财务上代价高昂。巴尔说：\"由于资金问题，我们认为有生之年都看不到这\n次搬迁发生。\"\n【信号】because of 直陈原因。答案就藏在因果连词后面：because of the funding 用 because\nof 把\"看不到搬迁发生\"的原因直接点成\"资金\"，再加上前句 It will be costly financially，\n原因句的因果信号非常明确，听到 because of 后那个词就是答案。\n【替换】It will be costly financially to the community ↔ It costs too much money.\ncostly financially 改写成 costs too much money\nbecause of the funding ↔ It costs too much money.\nfunding 印证钱是障碍\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音说\"有生之年看不到\"（in our lifetime）听着像\"耗时长\"，A 用 takes too long a time\n蹭这个时间感，但 in our lifetime 是搬不成的\"结果\"而非\"原因\"，真正原因是钱\n（funding），不是时间。\n借用原文：We don't see the move happening in our lifetime\nC) 未提及\n录音从头到尾没提\"州政府最终审批\"，C 的 wait for the state's final approval 是靠\"政府\n工程要审批\"的常识脑补的；虽然录音里有 state（the state's mainland），但那指\"州的大\n陆\"，与\"审批\"无关。\nD) 原词陷阱\n投票是 89 to 78，反对票确实不少，D 用 strong opposition from many villagers 蹭这个\n接近的票数，但录音明说搬不成是 because of the funding（资金），从未说是村民反对挡\n了路，把票数差错当成搬迁受阻的原因。\n借用原文：in a vote of 89 to 78", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What is the purpose of the social experiment?", "options": ["A) To investigate whether people are grateful for help.", "B) To see whether people hold doors open for strangers.", "C) To explore ways of inducing gratitude in people.", "D) To find out how people express gratitude."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （目的题） To investigate whether people are grateful for help.\n【定位】A man in Halifax, Canada, wanted to find out if people were thankful for someone holding\nthe door open for them.\n加拿大哈利法克斯的一名男子,想弄清楚人们会不会因为有人为自己开门而心怀感激。\n【信号】意图句 wanted to find out。录音开头 wanted to find out if people were thankful 用\nwanted to find out(想弄清楚)直白交代做实验的意图,这种\"想……/为了弄清……\"的意图句\n就是目的题的答案落点。\n【替换】wanted to find out if people were thankful for someone holding the door open ↔\nTo investigate whether people are grateful for help\n近义词整句替换\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 盗用了录音反复出现的 opening doors 与 hold the door open 字眼,但开门只是实验\"手\n段\",不是\"目的\";题问 purpose,把手段当目的就答非所问了。\n借用原文：A simple way to induce gratitude in people is opening doors\nC) 偷换概念\nC 来自 A simple way to induce gratitude in people is opening doors,录音确实说了\"诱\n发感激的方法\",但那是说话人解释\"为什么用开门\",是手段的理由,不是这场实验本身要\"查\n明\"的目的;实验目的是看人们会不会感激,而非\"探索诱发感激的方式\"。\n借用原文：A simple way to induce gratitude in people is opening doors and so we went to\nsix places\nD) 偷换概念\nD 说目的是\"查明人们如何表达感激\"(how people express),但录音问的是人们\"会不会\"感激\n(if people were thankful),是\"是否\"而非\"如何\";把\"是否感激\"偷换成\"如何表达\",方向变了。\n借用原文：wanted to find out if people were thankful", "points": ["听力·长对话", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What did Steve Foran and his team do in the experiment?", "options": ["A) They induced strangers to talk with them.", "B) They helped 15 to 20 people in a bad mood.", "C) They held doors open for people at various places.", "D) They interviewed people who didn't say thank you."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They held doors open for people at various places.\n【定位】For the experiment, Foran's team went to a shopping center, a mall, two office buildings, and\na coffee shop. The door was held for 15 to 20 people at a time at each location.\n为了这场实验,福兰的团队去了一家购物中心、一家商场、两栋办公楼和一家咖啡店。在每个\n地点,每次为 15 到 20 个人开门。\n【信号】行动叙述句 went to…held。Foran's team went to a shopping center, a mall, two\noffice buildings, and a coffee shop 加上 The door was held for 15 to 20 people,直接\n叙述团队的实际行动(去多处、开门),细节题答案就落在这种动作叙述句上。\n【替换】The door was held for 15 to 20 people ↔ They held doors open for people\n被动转主动\nwent to a shopping center, a mall, two office buildings, and a coffee shop ↔ at various places\n列举概括成上位词\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说\"诱使陌生人和他们交谈\",看似呼应录音 People got into conversations with us,但录\n音是\"人们(主动)和我们攀谈起来\",并非团队\"induce(诱使)\"他们说话;把自然发生说成团队刻\n意为之,动作主体和性质都变了。\n借用原文：People got into conversations with us\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 把录音原数 15 to 20 people 和后文 having a bad day 拼到一起,说团队\"帮了 15 到 20\n个心情不好的人\"。但 15 到 20 是\"每次开门服务的人数\",bad day 是说话人事后的猜测,两\n者本不相干,被强行黏成一句,与原意不符。\n借用原文：The door was held for 15 to 20 people at a time\nD) 未提及\nD 说团队\"采访了没道谢的人\",借用录音 We did have one that didn't say thank you 的字\n眼,但录音只是说\"有一个人没道谢\",且明说 We're not here to judge them,根本没提\"采访\n(interview)\"这个动作,属无中生有。\n借用原文：We did have one that didn't say thank you", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What do we learn from the news report?", "options": ["A) People can be educated to be grateful.", "B) Most people express gratitude for help.", "C) Most people have bad days now and then.", "D) People are ungrateful when in a bad mood."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （主旨题） Most people express gratitude for help.\n【定位】The social experiment showed that 99 out of 100 people expressed gratitude.\n这场社会实验显示,100 个人里有 99 个表达了感激。\n【信号】核心数据 99 out of 100。The social experiment showed that 99 out of 100 people\nexpressed gratitude 用\"实验显示\"引出 99/100 这个核心结果数据,这种\"实验/数据表明\"的\n句子承载整篇结论,是结论题的落点。\n【替换】99 out of 100 people expressed gratitude ↔ Most people express gratitude for help\n具体比例概括为 most\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nA 说\"人可以被教育得懂感恩\",像是从 induce gratitude in people(诱发感激)推来,但录音只\n说开门能\"诱发\"感激,没说人能\"被教育(educated)\"成感恩的人;从\"诱发\"跳到\"教育\"是多走了\n一步的推断。\n借用原文：A simple way to induce gratitude in people is opening doors\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 说\"大多数人时不时心情不好\",借用录音 having a bad day 的字眼,但录音只是说\"那个没\n道谢的人也许那天心情不好\",是对\"个别人\"的猜测,不是整篇结论;把个别推测说成\"大多数\n人\"的普遍现象,以偏概全。\n借用原文：there were probably a bunch of them having a bad day\nD) 与原文相反\nD 说\"人们心情不好时就不感激\",但报道的结论恰恰相反——99/100 都感激了,说话人还强调\ngrateful people make people grateful;把\"绝大多数会感激\"反成\"心情差就不感激\",方向完\n全拧了。\n借用原文：grateful people make people grateful", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What is the man's purpose for calling the woman?", "options": ["A) To order a solar panel installation.", "B) To report a serious leak in his roof.", "C) To enquire about solar panel installations.", "D) To complain about the faulty solar panels."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （目的题） To enquire about solar panel installations.\n【定位】Hi, my name is Winston. I wish to enquire about solar panel installations.\n你好，我叫 Winston。我想咨询一下太阳能板的安装。\n【信号】主动方自报来意句。对话一接通，主动打电话的人 Winston 用 I wish to enquire about\nsolar panel installations 一句话交代来意——这种\"自报家门 + 说明来意\"的句子就是目的\n题的答案信号，不必再往后听。\n【替换】I wish to enquire about solar panel installations ↔ To enquire about solar panel installations.\n陈述句改不定式、近乎原句\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项借了录音反复出现的 installation 一词造\"To order…installation\"，但 Winston 全程只\n说 I'm considering it\"在考虑\"，从没下单。听到 installation 就选 order 正中圈套。\n借用原文：Yes, I'm considering it.\nB) 未提及\n整段对话只谈咨询、省钱、价格，从未出现\"漏水\"或\"报修\"，report a serious leak 是凭空\n捏造，属预读时就该排除的反方向干扰。\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项借 solar panels 一词造\"complain about the faulty solar panels\"，可 Winston 是潜\n在新客户、从无抱怨，faulty（有故障的）更是录音没有的。听到 solar panels 就选投诉是\n被原词牵着走。\n借用原文：Yes, I'm considering it.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What do we learn about the man's neighbor from the conversation?", "options": ["A) He plans to install solar panels.", "B) He owns a four-bedroom house.", "C) He saves $300 a year.", "D) He has a large family."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He has a large family.\n【定位】My house is about the size you described, but my neighbor's is bigger. I'm not sure how many\npanels he has up there, but he does have a large family of six.\n我家差不多就是你说的那个大小，但我邻居家更大。我不确定他屋顶上装了几块板，不过他\n家确实是个六口之家的大家庭。\n【信号】对比句后落在邻居身上。Winston 先把自家与邻居对比 my neighbor's is bigger，再用强\n调句 he does have a large family of six 点出邻居的确切信息——does 加重语气正是答案\n落点，明确这条事实属于\"邻居\"。\n【替换】he does have a large family of six ↔ He has a large family.\n具体\"六口\"概括成\"大家庭\"\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音里\"打算装板\"的人是 Winston 本人 I'm considering it；邻居是\"一年前已经装了\"。把\nWinston 的打算安到邻居头上，正是题干设的\"问谁\"陷阱。\n借用原文：Yes, I'm considering it.\nB) 原词陷阱\nfour-bedroom 出自店员举例的 the average four-bedroom house，是泛指的平均房型，\n且 Winston 说邻居家\"更大\"，邻居并非四居室。盗用 four-bedroom 原词张冠李戴。\n借用原文：the average four-bedroom house\nC) 原词陷阱\n$300 出自店员的概算 save a family of four around \\$300 a year，针对的是\"四口之\n家\"的假设；邻居省的是 over \\$500。把 $300 套到邻居头上是数字张冠李戴。\n借用原文：save a family of four around \\$300 a year", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What is one of the man's chief concerns?", "options": ["A) The service of the solar panel company.", "B) The cost of a solar panel installation.", "C) The maintenance of the solar panels.", "D) The quality of the solar panels."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） The cost of a solar panel installation.\n【定位】But just quickly, if you don't mind, could you tell me approximately how much a typical\ninstallation costs, like, say, four panels?\n不过，不介意的话，能不能快速告诉我，一套典型的安装大概要多少钱，比如说，四块板？\n【信号】主动发问句问价格。Winston 用 could you tell me approximately how much a typical\ninstallation costs 主动开口追问\"多少钱\"——这种\"他专门发问\"的句子就是 chief concern\n的落点，how much 直指费用。\n【替换】how much a typical installation costs ↔ The cost of a solar panel installation.\nhow much costs 名词化为 the cost\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n店员说 we could show you the different solutions we offer 沾到\"服务\"，但那是店员的\n提议，Winston 并未就服务专门发问，不构成他的 chief concern。听到\"提供方案\"就选服\n务是被次要信息带偏。\n借用原文：we could show you the different solutions we offer\nC) 未提及\n对话从头到尾没出现 maintenance 或保养相关内容，是凭\"装板会涉及维护\"的常识脑补出\n来的干扰项，应排除。\nD) 未提及\n录音没有谈\"质量\"，Winston 关心的是省钱与价钱，没就 quality 发问。该项同属凭常识脑\n补，未在对话出现。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "How long will it take a typical household to make back the initial investment?", "options": ["A) One year and a half.", "B) Less than four years.", "C) Roughly six years.", "D) About five years."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） About five years.\n【定位】But you know a typical household will make back that initial investment in about five years.\n不过你知道，一个典型家庭大约在五年内就能收回这笔初始投资。\n【信号】题干同词语境后的年数。店员说 a typical household will make back that initial\ninvestment in about five years——\"收回初始投资\"的语境后面紧跟的年数 in about five\nyears 就是答案信号，与题干 make back the initial investment 完全同框。\n【替换】in about five years ↔ About five years.\nabout five years 近原词复现\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音里 about a year ago 提到邻居一年前装板，选项把它扭成\"一年半回本\"，盗用\"一\n年\"这个数字却安错了语境，与回本无关。\n借用原文：about a year ago\nB) 未提及\n录音从未出现 four years 或\"不到四年\"的回本时长，是凭空捏造、又故意与正确的五年咬得\n很近来诱选，应排除。\nC) 未提及\n录音只说回本约五年，并无\"六年\"，roughly six years 同属与正确答案接近的捏造数字，专\n钓没听准约数的考生。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "Where is the conversation taking place?", "options": ["A) At a travel agency.", "B) At an Australian airport.", "C) At an airline transfer service.", "D) At a local transportation authority."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） At a travel agency.\n【定位】W: Oh, no problem. I'm interested in booking a holiday to Australia and wonder if you could\ntell me what deals you have.\n女：哦，没关系。我想订一趟去澳大利亚的度假行程，想问问你们都有什么优惠套餐。\n【信号】业务内容暴露场景。答案句不报地名，而靠业务定场景：女士说 booking a holiday to\nAustralia 又问 what deals you have，加上第 3 段男士问 Are you only looking for\nflights or a package holiday——\"订度假行程/卖套餐\"这套业务只可能发生在旅行社。\n【替换】booking a holiday to Australia ↔ At a travel agency\n办的业务对应办事机构\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 At an Australian airport. 盗用了录音里的 Australia 一词，但澳大利亚只是\"要去的目\n的地\"，对话此刻发生在出发地的旅行社，并非身处澳洲机场——听到 Australia 就选机场\n即中招。\n借用原文：a holiday to Australia\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 At an airline transfer service. 靠录音里的 flights 制造联想，但对话谈的是订整套度\n假产品，不是单纯办转机；录音从没提转机服务，属由原词外推过头。\n借用原文：only looking for flights\nD) 未提及\n选项 At a local transportation authority. 在录音中找不到任何对应，对话从头到尾是订旅\n游套餐，与地方交通管理部门毫无关系，属凭\"transport\"一词硬凑。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "Why is the woman interested in package holidays?", "options": ["A) She would be able to visit more scenic spots.", "B) She wanted to save as much money as possible.", "C) She would like to have everything taken care of.", "D) She wanted to spend more time with her family."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） She would like to have everything taken care of.\n【定位】W: Yeah, that sounds pretty good. I'm going with my family, so it would be nice to have\neverything taken care of.\n女：是的，那听起来挺不错的。我是和家人一起去，所以要是一切都能被安排妥当就好了。\n【信号】因果 so 引出真实理由。答案落在因果词 so 之后：女士先说和家人同行，紧接 so it would\nbe nice to have everything taken care of——so 后面这句才是她偏好套餐的真正原因，\n正是被问者亲口给出的应答。\n【替换】it would be nice to have everything taken care of ↔\nShe would like to have everything taken care of\n委婉愿望换成直陈愿望\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n选项 She would be able to visit more scenic spots. 靠录音里男士提的 day trips to\ndifferent places of interest 制造联想，但那是男士介绍套餐含什么、不是女士给的理由；\n女士的理由是\"省心\"，不是\"多看景点\"。\n借用原文：day trips to different places of interest\nB) 未提及\n选项 She wanted to save as much money as possible. 听着是订套餐的常见动机，但录\n音里女士全程没提钱、没提省钱，这是凭常识脑补出来的理由，原文无据。\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 She wanted to spend more time with her family. 盗用了女士说的 I'm going with\nmy family，但\"和家人一起去\"只是事实背景、是\"想省心\"的前提，并非她偏好套餐的理由\n本身——听到 family 就选即中招。\n借用原文：I'm going with my family", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "How long does the woman want to go for the holiday?", "options": ["A) Four days.", "B) Five days.", "C) One week.", "D) Two weeks."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Two weeks.\n【定位】W: Two weeks around Christmas time would be great.\n女：圣诞节前后去两周就很好了。\n【信号】被问者对时长的直接应答。第 7 段男士问 How long do you want to go for and what\ndates?，女士第 8 段直接答 Two weeks around Christmas time——这是被问者对\"去多\n久\"的正面回答，答案就落在回答方这句，不在后面男士报行程的数字里。\n【替换】Two weeks ↔ Two weeks\n时长原词直接对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Four days. 盗用男士报行程时的 the other four nights，但那是 14 晚里\"自选行\n程\"的四晚，是其中一段、不是总时长；听到 four 就选即落入数字陷阱。\n借用原文：the other four nights\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 Five days. 盗用 Five nights in Sydney and five nights in Melbourne 里的 five，但\n那是悉尼、墨尔本各住五晚的分段天数，与女士想去的两周总时长无关。\n借用原文：five nights in Sydney and five nights in Melbourne\nC) 未提及\n选项 One week. 录音并未念出\"一周\"，它靠 14 nights 让人误折算/误记；女士明确说的是\n两周，one week 既非原话也非她的意思。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the woman say she likes about the holiday package?", "options": ["A) Choosing some activities herself.", "B) Spending Christmas with Australians.", "C) Driving along the Great Ocean Road.", "D) Learning more about wine making."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Choosing some activities herself.\n【定位】W: That sounds great. It's good we can choose some activities ourselves.\n女：那听起来太棒了。我们能自己挑一些活动，这点很好。\n【信号】表态句 It's good… 点出喜好。答案落在女士的点赞句：It's good we can choose some\nactivities ourselves——It’s good 是明确的正面表态信号，她真正说喜欢的就是\"能自己挑\n活动\"这一点，而非前一段男士罗列的各项活动。\n【替换】we can choose some activities ourselves ↔ Choosing some activities herself\nwe…ourselves 概括为 herself\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 Spending Christmas with Australians. 借录音里的 Christmas time 制造联想，但女\n士只说圣诞前后去两周，从没说要和澳洲人一起过圣诞，是把时间点过度引申成活动。\n借用原文：Two weeks around Christmas time\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Driving along the Great Ocean Road. 几乎原样盗用男士介绍的 go for a drive\ndown the Great Ocean Road，但那是男士罗列的可选活动之一、不是女士说她喜欢的；\n她喜欢的是\"能自己挑\"这件事本身。\n借用原文：go for a drive down the Great Ocean Road\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Learning more about wine making. 盗用男士说的 make wine/葡萄酒之旅，同样是\n清单里的一项可选活动，女士没说喜欢酿酒，她点赞的是\"能自己选\"。\n借用原文：if you're interested in wine, you could go on a tour", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What would tourists have to do when they visit Indonesia's Bali Island?", "options": ["A) Bring their own bags when shopping.", "B) Use public transport when traveling.", "C) Dispose of their trash properly.", "D) Pay a green tax upon arrival."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Pay a green tax upon arrival.\n【定位】Tourists taking a holiday in the Indonesian island of Bali are facing a new $14-per-person tax\nwhen they arrive on the holiday island from next year. But this is a green tax.\n到印尼巴厘岛度假的游客，从明年起抵达这座度假岛屿时将面临一项每人 14 美元的新税。\n不过这是一项绿色税（环保税）。\n【信号】录音开头点题句。篇章独白往往开头第一句就把核心事实抛出来，本篇正是如此：Tourists\ntaking a holiday in the Indonesian island of Bali are facing a new \\$14-per-person\ntax when they arrive，紧接着 But this is a green tax 点明这税的性质。开篇就出现\"游客\n+ 抵达 + 交税\"，正对题干所问，是答案句。\n【替换】facing a new \\$14-per-person tax when they arrive ↔ Pay a green tax upon arrival\n面临交税=要交税\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Bring their own bags when shopping. 看似很环保、很合理，但原文从未要求游客\n购物自带袋；它只是利用 bags 这个全篇反复出现的词来诱你\"听到 bags 就选\"，属于答非\n所问。\n借用原文：ban single-use plastic bags\nB) 未提及\n选项 Use public transport when traveling. 在原文里完全没有对应内容，原文末尾提到的\n是 electric vehicles（电动车），与坐公共交通不是一回事，属于未提及。\nC) 答非所问\n选项 Dispose of their trash properly. 同样听起来很\"环保正确\"，但原文谈的是政府层面的\n征税、禁塑、清理环境（clean up the island's natural environment），并没有要求游客\n自己妥善处理垃圾，属答非所问。\n借用原文：clean up the island's natural environment", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does the passage say about Indonesia?", "options": ["A) It has not been doing a good job in recycling.", "B) It has witnessed a rise in accidental drowning.", "C) It has not attracted many tourists in recent years.", "D) It has experienced an overall decline in air quality."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It has not been doing a good job in recycling.\n【定位】Indonesia is drowning in plastics. Recycling is not one of the country's strong points.\n印尼正被塑料垃圾淹没。回收并不是这个国家的强项。\n【信号】对象名+负面定性句。录音里一出现题干对象 Indonesia is drowning in plastics，紧接的\nRecycling is not one of the country's strong points 就给出对印尼的明确评价——回收\n不是强项。带 not one of…strong points 的否定定性句，正是承载答案的信号句。\n【替换】Recycling is not one of the country's strong points ↔ not been doing a good job in recycling\n回收非强项=回收做得不好\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 It has witnessed a rise in accidental drowning. 盗用了原文 Indonesia is drowning\nin plastics 里的 drowning。原文 drowning in plastics 是\"被塑料淹没\"的比喻，与真人溺\n水（accidental drowning）毫无关系，是典型听到原词就上当的陷阱。\n借用原文：Indonesia is drowning in plastics\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 It has not attracted many tourists in recent years. 虽然原文确有 tourists 一词\n（Tourists taking a holiday in the Indonesian island of Bali），但原文只说游客来度\n假、要交税，从未说近年游客变少，属未提及。\n借用原文：Tourists taking a holiday in the Indonesian island of Bali\nD) 未提及\n选项 It has experienced an overall decline in air quality. 讲空气质量下降，但全篇只谈\n塑料、回收、水道（protect the island's waterways），从头到尾没提空气，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What is the new plan Governor Koster recently announced?", "options": ["A) To charge a small fee on plastic products in supermarkets.", "B) To ban single-use plastic bags and straws on Bali Island.", "C) To promote the use of paper bags for shopping.", "D) To impose a penalty on anyone caught littering."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） To ban single-use plastic bags and straws on Bali Island.\n【定位】A few months ago, Koster announced a plan that would not only ban single-use plastic bags\nfrom supermarkets and convenience stores, but plastic bags and straws across the island.\n几个月前，Koster 宣布了一项计划，不仅要禁止超市和便利店里的一次性塑料袋，还要禁止\n全岛范围内的塑料袋和吸管。\n【信号】announced a plan标志句。题干问 new plan…announced，录音里 Koster announced\na plan 就是对应的标志句；其后 not only…but…结构 ban single-use plastic bags…but\nplastic bags and straws across the island 把计划内容铺开。听到 announced a plan 就\n该锁定这句。\n【替换】ban single-use plastic bags…but plastic bags and straws across the island ↔\nban single-use plastic bags and straws on Bali Island\n全岛禁塑袋吸管=岛上禁\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 To charge a small fee on plastic products in supermarkets. 借了原文\nsupermarkets 一词（ban single-use plastic bags from supermarkets and\nconvenience stores），但新计划是 ban（禁用）不是 charge a fee（收费），把治理动\n词偷换了，是收费 vs 禁用的细微差别陷阱。\n借用原文：ban single-use plastic bags from supermarkets and convenience stores\nC) 未提及\n选项 To promote the use of paper bags for shopping. 用了和 bags、shopping 同主题\n的词，但原文只字未提推广纸袋，新计划是禁塑而非倡导用纸袋，属未提及。\nD) 答非所问\n选项 To impose a penalty on anyone caught littering. 是另一种治理手段（罚乱扔垃\n圾），原文虽提到 Koster 还有别的护水道、推电动车的法规（he has more laws\nplanned），但本题问的\"最近宣布的这项新计划\"是禁塑，不是罚乱扔，属答非所问。\n借用原文：he has more laws planned", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about the North Atlantic right whale?", "options": ["A) It gives birth to several babies at a time.", "B) It is the least protected mammal species.", "C) It's breeding grounds are now better preserved.", "D) It's population is now showing signs of increase."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It's population is now showing signs of increase.\n【定位】That brings the number seen in nearby waters alone this year to three. That's big news\nbecause the whale population has been falling, and no baby whales were seen last year. In all,\nseven baby whales have been spotted so far this year.\n这使得仅在附近水域今年看到的（幼鲸）数量达到了三头。这是个大新闻，因为此前鲸鱼数\n量一直在下降、去年一头幼鲸都没见到。算下来，今年到目前已经发现了七头幼鲸。\n【信号】big news 加数字对比。信号是强调性短语加前后数字对比：原文说 That's big news\nbecause the whale population has been falling, and no baby whales were seen last\nyear，用 big news（大新闻）点出这是个值得注意的转好，再用\"去年一头幼鲸都没有\"对\n比\"今年发现七头\"，正面变化被强调出来——被加重语气和数字对比点亮的这句就是答\n案句。\n【替换】seven baby whales have been spotted so far this year ↔\npopulation is now showing signs of increase\n具体数字概括为数量回升\n【排除】A) 过度推断\n原文提到一头母鲸带着 a mum with two babies（一只母鲸带两个幼崽），听到 two\nbabies 容易让人以为\"一次生好几个\"，但\"带着两个幼崽\"不等于\"一胎产数仔\"，原文没说生\n育方式，属于把零散信息脑补成 A 项。\n借用原文：a mum with two babies\nB) 原词陷阱\n原文确实多次提到保护/濒危（如下文讲\"靠近它们是非法的\"），It is the least protected\nmammal species. 借\"保护\"这个话题词来诱选，但原文从没说它是\"最不受保护的\"哺乳动\n物，least（最不）这个极端程度是选项自己加的，与原文不符。\n借用原文：It's illegal to get within 1,500 feet of the animals without a federal research permit\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文提到鲸鱼在东南海岸 give birth off the Southeast Coast of America（繁殖产崽），\nIt's breeding grounds are now better preserved. 借\"繁殖地\"这个话题词诱选，但原文只\n说它在哪里繁殖、迁去哪里觅食，从没说\"繁殖地被更好地保护了\"，better preserved 是无\n中生有。\n借用原文：The whales give birth off the Southeast Coast of America in the winter", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What has caused the decline of the whale population in recent years?", "options": ["A) Global warming.", "B) Polluted seawaters.", "C) Commercial hunting.", "D) Decreasing birthrates."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Commercial hunting.\n【定位】The whale population has become endangered due to commercial whaling activities in recent\nyears. This is because they are sometimes hunted for their meat or their skin.\n近年来，由于商业捕鲸活动，鲸鱼种群已变得濒危。这是因为它们有时会因肉或皮而遭到\n猎杀。\n【信号】因果信号 due to 加 because。信号是连续两个因果标志：原文 has become\nendangered due to commercial whaling activities 用 due to（由于）直接给出原因，紧\n接着 This is because they are sometimes hunted 又用 because 进一步解释——因果信\n号词扎堆出现的这句，就是原因题的答案句。\n【替换】due to commercial whaling activities ↔ Commercial hunting\n捕鲸等于商业猎杀\n【排除】A) 未提及\nGlobal warming. 全球变暖是动物濒危话题里最\"想当然\"的原因，但通篇原文只字未提变\n暖，它是利用考生常识设的坑——凭\"濒危动物多半是因为气候\"的惯性去选，正好中招。\nB) 未提及\nPolluted seawaters. 海水污染同样是海洋动物受害的\"标准答案式\"原因，听上去合理，但原\n文从头到尾没有提到污染，是又一个用常识诱选、原文无据的干扰。\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文提过 no baby whales were seen last year（去年没见到幼鲸），Decreasing\nbirthrates. 出生率下降借这点诱选，但原文是把数量下降归因于商业捕鲸，从没说\"出生率\n下降\"是原因；\"去年没见到幼鲸\"是数量下降的表现，不是题目问的原因，属于因果错位。\n借用原文：no baby whales were seen last year", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "Why do the whales travel to the Northeast Coast of America in the early spring?", "options": ["A) To mate.", "B) To look for food.", "C) To escape hunters.", "D) To seek breeding grounds."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （目的题） To look for food.\n【定位】The whales give birth off the Southeast Coast of America in the winter and travel to feeding\ngrounds off the Northeast Coast in the early spring. The Northeast Coast is a critically\nimportant source of food.\n鲸鱼冬天在美国东南海岸产崽，早春则迁往东北海岸的觅食区。东北海岸是一处极其重要的\n食物来源。\n【信号】feeding grounds 加 source of food。信号是\"动作去某地 + 紧接的解释\"：原文先说\ntravel to feeding grounds off the Northeast Coast（迁往东北海岸的觅食区），下一句\n又补 The Northeast Coast is a critically important source of food（东北海岸是极重要\n的食物来源），两处 feeding/food 反复点出目的就是觅食——这就是目的题的答案落点。\n【替换】travel to feeding grounds off the Northeast Coast ↔ To look for food\n觅食区即为找食物\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n原文确实提到鲸鱼 give birth off the Southeast Coast（在东南海岸产崽），To mate. 交\n配借这点诱选，但产崽/交配发生在东南海岸的冬天，而题目问的是早春迁往东北海岸的目\n的，地点和季节都对不上，属于张冠李戴。\n借用原文：give birth off the Southeast Coast of America in the winter\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文多处提到 hunt/hunters（如 hunted for their meat、whale watchers），To escape\nhunters. 躲避猎人借这个反复出现的耳熟词诱选，但原文从没说鲸鱼迁往东北海岸是为了躲\n猎人，迁徙目的明明白白是觅食，hunters 只是别处话题，与本题目的无关。\n借用原文：they are sometimes hunted for their meat or their skin\nD) 偷换概念\n原文提到鲸鱼在东南海岸产崽繁殖，To seek breeding grounds. 寻找繁殖地借\"繁殖\"这一\n话题词诱选，但繁殖是在东南海岸、冬天的事，迁往东北海岸是为了 feeding grounds（觅\n食区）而非 breeding grounds（繁殖地），选项把\"觅食\"偷换成了\"繁殖\"。\n借用原文：travel to feeding grounds off the Northeast Coast", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the passage say about Americans?", "options": ["A) They prefer to drink low-fat milk.", "B) They think milk is good for health.", "C) They consume less milk these days.", "D) They buy more milk than the British."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They consume less milk these days.\n【定位】Americans drink 37% less milk than they did in the 1970s.\n美国人喝的牛奶比上世纪七十年代少了百分之三十七。\n【信号】对象名+数字下降，直接应答句。这句以 Americans 开头直接讲美国人，又带具体数字\n37% less milk，是对 What about Americans 的直接事实陈述，数字 + 比较（than they\ndid in the 1970s）就是答案信号。\n【替换】Americans drink 37% less milk than they did in the 1970s ↔ They consume less milk these days\ndrink less=consume less，把具体数字概括为更少\n【排除】A) 未提及\nprefer to drink low-fat milk 里的 low-fat（低脂）在录音中根本没出现，是凭对牛奶的常\n识臆造的好听说法。\nB) 与原文相反\nthink milk is good for health 看似呼应原文 what was once advertised as nutritious，\n但原文紧接着说 is becoming unpopular，即如今观念已转向负面，选项却说成正面看\n法，正话反说。\n借用原文：but what was once advertised as nutritious is becoming unpopular\nD) 张冠李戴\nbuy more milk than the British 盗用原文出现过的 in the UK（英国），但原文只是分别\n说美国和英国的消费都在下降，从未把两国相比、更没说美国买得更多，属张冠李戴式的虚\n构比较。\n借用原文：and in the UK, dairy consumption overall has fallen by a third in the past 20 years", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "How do Americans and British people think of milk nowadays?", "options": ["A) It is not as healthy as once thought.", "B) It is not easy to stay fresh for long.", "C) It benefits the elderly more.", "D) It tends to make people fat."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） It is not as healthy as once thought.\n【定位】But what was once advertised as nutritious is becoming unpopular. ... Milk is increasingly\nbeing described in a negative light.\n但曾经被宣传为有营养的牛奶，如今正变得不受欢迎。……牛奶正越来越多地被以负面的方\n式描述。\n【信号】转折 But 与 negative 定调。But what was once advertised as nutritious is becoming\nunpopular 由转折词 But 引出当下态度，再由 increasingly being described in a\nnegative light 用 negative 把如今的看法定调为负面——转折后 + 负面评价词就是态度题\n的答案信号。\n【替换】what was once advertised as nutritious is becoming unpopular ↔\nIt is not as healthy as once thought\n曾被当营养、如今转负=不如以前以为的健康\n【排除】B) 未提及\nnot easy to stay fresh for long 谈牛奶保鲜，录音通篇讲营养、消化与态度，从未提保\n鲜，属凭生活常识硬塞的内容。\nC) 未提及\nbenefits the elderly more 说更利老人，录音只在后半提到牛奶有营养、有益处，从没说对\n老人益处更大，属无中生有的程度拔高。\nD) 与原文相反\ntends to make people fat 迎合喝奶会胖的常识，但原文后半明确说 isn't full of calories\n（热量并不高），与发胖说法相反。\n借用原文：isn't full of calories", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "Why does drinking milk cause pain in some people?", "options": ["A) They drink too many pints every day.", "B) They are sensitive to certain minerals.", "C) They lack the necessary proteins to digest it.", "D) They have eaten food incompatible with milk."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They lack the necessary proteins to digest it.\n【定位】It needs a series of complex proteins to break it down. Without enough of these proteins, the\nlactose is broken down by bacteria in the human body. This can cause physical pain and\nproduce gas in the stomach.\n乳糖需要一系列复杂的蛋白质来分解它。如果这些蛋白质不够，乳糖就会被人体内的细菌分\n解，这会引起身体疼痛并在胃里产生气体。\n【信号】Without enough proteins…cause pain 的因果链。Without enough of these\nproteins 引出缺蛋白质这一条件，紧接 This can cause physical pain 给出疼痛后果——缺\n蛋白质→疼痛的因果链就是答案信号。\n【替换】Without enough of these proteins, the lactose is broken down by bacteria ↔\nThey lack the necessary proteins to digest it\nwithout enough proteins=lack the necessary proteins\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\ndrink too many pints every day 盗用原文开头的 144 pints of milk a year 里的 pints，\n但那只是讲平均消费量，原文从没说喝太多导致疼痛，属答非所问。\n借用原文：An average person consumes 144 pints of milk a year\nB) 偷换概念\nsensitive to certain minerals 把原文真正的关键词 proteins（蛋白质）偷换成 minerals\n（矿物质），原文谈的是缺蛋白质，与对矿物质敏感无关。\n借用原文：It needs a series of complex proteins to break it down\nD) 未提及\neaten food incompatible with milk 说吃了与牛奶相克的食物，录音只讲缺蛋白质、细菌\n分解乳糖，从未提食物相克，属凭民间说法臆造。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does the passage say is a benefit of milk?", "options": ["A) It is easier for sick people to digest.", "B) It provides some necessary nutrients.", "C) It is healthier than other animal products.", "D) It supplies the body with enough calories."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It provides some necessary nutrients.\n【定位】Despite the problems in digesting milk, it does provide many benefits. Milk is nutritious. It\ncontains vitamins A and D, as well as protein and isn't full of calories.\n尽管牛奶存在消化方面的问题，它确实提供许多益处。牛奶很有营养，含有维生素 A 和 D\n以及蛋白质，而且热量并不高。\n【信号】Despite 转回好处，benefits/nutritious 定调。Despite the problems in digesting\nmilk, it does provide many benefits 用 Despite 把话题从缺点转回好处，再以 Milk is\nnutritious 和列举的维生素、蛋白质点明营养——转折回正面 + benefits/nutritious 就是\n答案信号。\n【替换】Milk is nutritious. It contains vitamins A and D, as well as protein ↔\nIt provides some necessary nutrients\n列举的维生素蛋白质=概括的 necessary nutrients\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\neasier for sick people to digest 盗用原文结尾的 If you get sick in your stomach 里的\nsick 和前文的 digest，但原文是说不舒服可自测缺蛋白质，从没说牛奶更易被病人消化，属\n答非所问。\n借用原文：If you get sick in your stomach within the next 24 hours, you are lacking in the proteins\nto digest milk\nC) 未提及\nhealthier than other animal products 拿牛奶与其他动物制品比，录音只说牛奶本身有营\n养，从没和其他动物制品作比较，属无中生有的比较。\nD) 与原文相反\nsupplies the body with enough calories 盗用原文的 calories，但原文说的是 isn't full of\ncalories（热量并不高），选项却说提供足够热量，把否定改成了肯定，与原文相反。\n借用原文：isn't full of calories", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) choose", "B) constant", "C) disappointing", "D) distinguish", "E) exhausting", "F) experienced", "G) negative", "H) outcome", "I) pattern", "J) plural", "K) repeatedly", "L) rewarded", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) undertaken"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) constant\n形容词\n【词性槽】from + ___ + 名词 praise，定语位\n【依据】对应下文\"反复使用\"\"过度表扬\"——\"持续不断的表扬\"\nover-praising can have a __(27)__ effect 与 When we use the same praise __(28)__ 都\n在说\"表扬太多/太频繁\"，故空里要的是\"持续不断的\"——constant praise 完美对应主旨\"过\n度表扬反成害\"。\n【竞争词】C) disappointing \"令人失望的表扬\"逻辑反了\ndisappointing 表\"令人失望\"，下文说孩子从表扬中\"得不到我们想象的那么多益处\"，原因是\"太\n多\"而非\"令人失望\"，且 C 是第 30 题的钩子（fear of disappointing their parents）。\nE) exhausting \"令人筋疲力尽的表扬\"不通\nexhausting 多修饰 work/journey 等耗体力的事，不会修饰 praise；且与下文\"过度表扬使表扬\n变空洞\"的因果对不上。\nF) experienced experienced praise 不是英语搭配\nexperienced 用来修饰人（有经验的医生/老师），不修饰 praise；语义层面也讲不通\"有经验的\n表扬\"。\nG) negative \"负面表扬\"自相矛盾，且 G 是 27 题的钩子\nnegative praise 自身就矛盾——表扬本身是正面的。同时 27 题 over-praising can have a\n__(27)__ effect 才是 negative 的天然落点。\nJ) plural \"复数的表扬\"不通\nplural 是语法术语\"复数的\"，与表扬的频率/性质完全无关，属于明显干扰词。\nL) rewarded rewarded praise 不成搭配，且 L 是 29 题钩子\nrewarded 是\"被奖赏的\"，修饰\"表扬\"本身不通顺；本词更适合 29 题 anything they do must\nbe __(29)__ with praise。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 G) —— G) negative 在 26 题极有迷惑性——词性也对、感情色彩\n也沾边。考生看到 children don't benefit from __(26)__ praise 容易直接选 negative（负\n面的表扬），但下文 over-praising can have a __(27)__ effect 里的 effect 才是 negative\n的正确落点；26 题需要的是\"持续不断的\"，对应主旨\"过度表扬\"。防坑——先把整段读\n完、看到 27 题的 However 再回填 26，能避免抢词。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) choose", "B) constant", "C) disappointing", "D) distinguish", "E) exhausting", "F) experienced", "G) negative", "H) outcome", "I) pattern", "J) plural", "K) repeatedly", "L) rewarded", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) undertaken"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) negative\n形容词\n【词性槽】冠词 a 与名词 effect 之间，定语位\n【依据】与上文\"父母以为表扬利于自信\"形成 However 转折\nParents often praise, believing they are building their child's self-confidence.\nHowever, over-praising can have a __(27)__ effect：However 提示语义反转，前句是正\n面（建立自信），所以 effect 应是反面——negative effect\"负面效应\"是固定搭配，逻辑\n严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】C) disappointing C 是 30 题钩子；这里 negative 更直接更常见\ndisappointing effect 偶尔可见，但 negative effect 是绝对高频且与 However 转折语义最对\n位；同时 C 在 30 题 fear of __(30)__ their parents 才是正确落点。\nE) exhausting \"令人筋疲力尽的影响\"与上下文无关\nexhausting effect 多用于体力/精神耗损语境，与\"过度表扬使表扬贬值\"的因果完全对不上。\nF) experienced experienced effect 不成搭配\nexperienced 修饰人，不修饰 effect；语义上也不通。\nJ) plural \"复数的影响\"语法术语，与本文无关\nplural 是\"复数形式\"，纯语法概念，与教育心理学的语境无关。\nL) rewarded rewarded effect 不成搭配\nrewarded 不能修饰 effect；本词更适合 29 题的 be rewarded with 搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) choose", "B) constant", "C) disappointing", "D) distinguish", "E) exhausting", "F) experienced", "G) negative", "H) outcome", "I) pattern", "J) plural", "K) repeatedly", "L) rewarded", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) undertaken"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) repeatedly\n副词\n【词性槽】动词 use 后修饰动作 → 要副词\n【依据】对应上文\"使用同样的表扬\"暗含的\"反复\"\nWhen we use the same praise __(28)__, it may become empty：\"反复地\"用同一句表\n扬，才会让表扬变得空洞无意义；repeatedly 与 the same praise、become empty 三者\n构成完整因果链。\n【竞争词】M) separately \"分开地\"与上下文逻辑无关\nseparately 表示\"分别、单独\"，但句子讨论的是\"同一句表扬反复使用\"导致贬值，不存在\"分开使\n用\"的语义，逻辑对不上。\nN) simply \"仅仅\"无法解释\"变空洞\"的原因\nsimply 表示\"仅仅、只不过\"，常作语气副词。但本句要解释表扬为何 become empty，需要的\n是\"次数/频率\"信息，simply 给不出这个信息，且 N 在 34 题有更典型的用武之地（提前预判用\n过即划）。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) choose", "B) constant", "C) disappointing", "D) distinguish", "E) exhausting", "F) experienced", "G) negative", "H) outcome", "I) pattern", "J) plural", "K) repeatedly", "L) rewarded", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) undertaken"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) rewarded\n形容词\n【词性槽】be + ___ + with：被动结构里要过去分词作表语\n【依据】be rewarded with 是\"被奖以……\"的固定搭配\nanything they do must be __(29)__ with praise：\"他们做的任何事都必须以表扬作为奖\n励\"，be rewarded with praise 是地道搭配，完美对应上下文\"孩子开始期待每件事都得到\n表扬\"。\n【竞争词】C) disappointing be disappointing with 不是英语搭配\ndisappointing 是主动义\"令人失望的\"，be disappointing with 不成立；且 C 在 30 题才是正\n确落点。\nE) exhausting be exhausting with 不成搭配\nexhausting 是\"令人筋疲力尽的\"，与 with praise 完全无关；语义也不通。\nF) experienced be experienced with X 是\"对 X 有经验\"，主语应是人\nbe experienced with 的主语只能是人，而本句主语是 anything they do（事），逻辑不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) choose", "B) constant", "C) disappointing", "D) distinguish", "E) exhausting", "F) experienced", "G) negative", "H) outcome", "I) pattern", "J) plural", "K) repeatedly", "L) rewarded", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) undertaken"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) disappointing\n动词\n【词性槽】fear of + 动名词：of 后接 V-ing\n【依据】fear of disappointing their parents = \"怕让父母失望\"\nthe child avoiding taking risks due to fear of __(30)__ their parents：上文说孩子被表\n扬养成依赖，做事就怕没表扬；逻辑链 = 怕没被表扬 → 怕让父母失望 → 不敢冒险。\ndisappointing 表\"使……失望\"，逻辑完美闭环。\n【竞争词】E) exhausting exhausting their parents = \"使父母疲惫\"语义不符\nexhaust 表\"使筋疲力尽\"，与上下文\"孩子怕得不到表扬、不敢冒险\"的心理逻辑无关；本句要表\n达的是怕父母不开心，不是怕父母累。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) choose", "B) constant", "C) disappointing", "D) distinguish", "E) exhausting", "F) experienced", "G) negative", "H) outcome", "I) pattern", "J) plural", "K) repeatedly", "L) rewarded", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) undertaken"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) outcome\n名词\n【词性槽】rather than 后与前面 the process 平行 → 名词\n【依据】\"过程 vs 结果\"是教育心理学经典对立\nfocus on the process rather than the __(31)__：process（过程）的天然反义就是\noutcome（结果）；下文 It is the recognition of a child's attempt, or the process in\nwhich they achieved something, that is essential 也是在强调\"过程\"对立\"结果\"。\n【竞争词】I) pattern pattern 与 process 不构成反义对立\npattern 表\"模式、惯常做法\"，process 表\"过程\"，二者并非常见反义；且 I 在 32 题 the\n__(32)__ of praise we're all so accustomed to 才是天然落点（习惯化的\"模式\"）。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) choose", "B) constant", "C) disappointing", "D) distinguish", "E) exhausting", "F) experienced", "G) negative", "H) outcome", "I) pattern", "J) plural", "K) repeatedly", "L) rewarded", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) undertaken"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) pattern\n名词\n【词性槽】the + ___ + of 名词 = 名词中心词\n【依据】\"打破表扬的（习惯化）模式\"——pattern 表惯常做法\nhow do we break the __(32)__ of praise we're all so accustomed to：we're all so\naccustomed to 提示这是\"我们都已习惯的\"东西，pattern 正是\"惯常模式\"；break the\npattern 是固定搭配\"打破惯例\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) choose", "B) constant", "C) disappointing", "D) distinguish", "E) exhausting", "F) experienced", "G) negative", "H) outcome", "I) pattern", "J) plural", "K) repeatedly", "L) rewarded", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) undertaken"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) distinguish\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 important to 后接动词原形\n【依据】固定搭配 distinguish between A and B = 区分两者\nto __(33)__ between \"person praise\" and \"process praise\"：英语中\"区分 A 和 B\"的标\n准搭配就是 distinguish between A and B；下文紧接着分别给两者下定义，正是\"区分\"的\n展开。\n【竞争词】A) choose choose between 是\"在两者中选一个\"，与下文\"两者都解释\"矛盾\nchoose between A and B 意为\"在 A 和 B 中选一个\"，而下文 Phillip 同时定义 person praise\n与 process praise、并未让人二选一，因此 choose 逻辑不符。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 A) —— A) choose 与 between 搭配也成立，是 33 题最经典陷\n阱。to __(33)__ between \"person praise\" and \"process praise\" 表面看 choose\nbetween 完全成立；但下文紧接着同时给两个 praise 下定义，并非让人二选一。防坑——\n看 between 后下文是\"选一个\"还是\"分清楚\"，本段是后者，故 distinguish。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) choose", "B) constant", "C) disappointing", "D) distinguish", "E) exhausting", "F) experienced", "G) negative", "H) outcome", "I) pattern", "J) plural", "K) repeatedly", "L) rewarded", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) undertaken"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) simply\n副词\n【词性槽】be 动词与现在分词之间 → 副词作状语\n【依据】\"仅仅是说某人多棒\"——是给 person praise 下定义\nPerson praise is __(34)__ saying how great someone is. It's a form of personal\napproval.：simply 表\"仅仅、只不过\"，用来给概念下精简的定义最贴切：\"person praise\n不过就是夸某人多棒\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) choose", "B) constant", "C) disappointing", "D) distinguish", "E) exhausting", "F) experienced", "G) negative", "H) outcome", "I) pattern", "J) plural", "K) repeatedly", "L) rewarded", "M) separately", "N) simply", "O) undertaken"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) undertaken\n动词\n【词性槽】has just + ___：现在完成时要过去分词\n【依据】undertake efforts = \"付出努力\"，是地道动宾搭配\nacknowledgement of the efforts the person has just __(35)__：undertake 表\"承担、从\n事\"，undertake efforts/a task 是常见搭配；\"承认这个人刚刚付出的努力\"——与上文\nprocess praise 关注\"过程\"完美呼应。\n【竞争词】F) experienced experience efforts 不是英语搭配\nexperience 常接 success/difficulty/loss 等\"经历到的事\"，不与 efforts 直接搭配；说\"经历了\n努力\"也不符合 process praise\"承认所做努力\"的核心义。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "One legislative staffer assumed that a woman of color who advocated affordable childcare must be a single mother.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】立法助理与单亲母亲的成见\n【定位】That comment, says Mothering Justice director Danielle Atkinson, \"was meant to shame\"\nand relied on the familiar notion that a woman of color concerned about income inequality\nand programs that promote mobility must by definition be a single mom, probably with\nmultiple kids.\nMothering Justice 主任 Danielle Atkinson 说，那番话'意在让人难堪'，依赖于一种熟悉的\n观念：一个关心收入不平等和促进流动性项目的有色人种女性，按定义必然是单亲妈妈，而\n且很可能带着好几个孩子。\n【改写】One legislative staffer ↔ A legislative staffer\n近乎原样复现\nassumed that a woman of color who advocated affordable childcare must be a single mother ↔\nrelied on the familiar notion that a woman of color concerned about income inequality and\nprograms that promote mobility must by definition be a single mom\nassumed=relied on the notion\n【辨邻】 D 该段虽提到a legislative staffer dismissed the activist's concerns，也是与立法助理\n的对话现场，但只引用了助理my husband took care of that—I stayed home这句私\n人说法，并未点明她'假设这位活动家是单亲母亲'——把成见上升为'单亲母亲'的判断\n是下一段才做的。\nA legislative staffer dismissed the activist's concerns, telling her \"my husband took care of that—I\nstayed home.\"", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "People from different races, genders and regions all suffer from a lack of financial security.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】种族/性别/地域三维并列\n【定位】But the fact that 4 in 10 Americans can't come up with $400 in an emergency is a\ncommonly cited statistic for good reason: economic instability stretches across race, gender,\nand geography.\n但是十分之四的美国人无法在紧急情况下拿出400美元这一事实是被频繁引用的数据，原因\n充分：经济不稳定横跨种族、性别和地域。\n【改写】different races, genders and regions ↔ across race, gender, and geography\n并列三元同义对位\nall suffer from a lack of financial security ↔ economic instability stretches across\n缺乏金融安全等于经济不稳\n【辨邻】 G 该段说poverty is extremely diverse并列举了children, the elderly, people with\ndisabilities, and people of color，谈的是'哪类群体更受影响'，并未陈述'种族/性别/\n地域三维都受影响'这一横跨维度的判断。\npoverty is extremely diverse. We know that some people are more affected than others, like\nchildren, the elderly, people with disabilities, and people of color.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "According to a survey, while the majority believe too little assistance is given to the poor, more than a third believe too much is spent on welfare.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】三分之一以上认为福利花太多\n【定位】According to the General Social Survey, 71 percent of respondents believe the country is\nspending too little on \"assistance to the poor\". On the other hand, 22 percent think we are\nspending too little on \"welfare\": 37 percent believe we are spending too much.\n根据综合社会调查，71%的受访者认为国家在'对穷人的援助'上花得太少。另一方面，22%\n认为'福利'花得太少，37%认为花得太多。\n【改写】the majority believe too little assistance is given to the poor ↔\n71 percent of respondents believe the country is spending too little on \"assistance to the poor\"\nmajority等于71%\nmore than a third believe too much is spent on welfare ↔\n37 percent believe we are spending too much\nmore than a third等于37%\n【辨邻】 F 该段数据38 million people、140 million people是穷人人口规模统计，并非a\nsurvey中关于'花得多少'的态度调查，对象与陈述句不一致。\nTaking into account economic need beyond that absolute measure, the Institute for Policy Studies\nfound that 140 million people are poor or low-income.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "A research group has found that Americans who are struggling are thought to be lazy and to have made the wrong decisions.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】研究机构定性挣扎者懒惰\n【定位】When we see people who are struggling,\" he says, those assumptions \"lead us to the\nperception that people in poverty are lazy, they don't care, and they haven't made the\nright decisions.\n他说，当我们看到正在挣扎的人时，那些假设'让我们形成这样的认知：贫困中的人懒惰、不\n在乎、没有做出正确的决定'。\n【改写】A research group has found ↔\nThe FrameWorks Institute, a research group that focuses on public framing of issues, has\nstudied\nresearch group原词复现\nAmericans who are struggling are thought to be lazy ↔\nWhen we see people who are struggling,\" he says, those assumptions \"lead us to the perception\nthat people in poverty are lazy\nare thought to be等于lead us to the perception\nhave made the wrong decisions ↔ haven't made the right decisions\nwrong等于haven't加right\n【辨邻】 K 该段承接J段继续引用Kendall-Taylor的话谈cognitive blindness，但没有给出'挣扎者\n被认为懒惰且做错了决定'这一研究结论本身，只是对前段结论的延伸评价。\nWhen people enter into that pattern of thinking, it's cognitively comfortable to make sense of issues\nof poverty in that way. It creates a kind of cognitive blindness", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Under the old system in America, a mother was supposed to stay home and take care of her children.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】旧体系下母亲在家\n【定位】One of the Mothering Justice organizers went to the office of a state representative to talk\nabout the lack of affordable childcare—the vestiges(痕迹) of a system that expected mothers\nto stay home with their children while their husbands worked.\nMothering Justice 的一位组织者去州众议员办公室谈论可负担育儿服务的缺失——那是一\n个体系的遗痕：这个体系预期母亲在家照看孩子，而丈夫外出工作。\n【改写】Under the old system in America ↔ the vestiges(痕迹) of a system\nvestiges暗示旧体系\na mother was supposed to stay home and take care of her children ↔\nexpected mothers to stay home with their children\nsupposed to与expected to同义", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "It was found that nearly 50% of Americans are poor or receive low pay.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】近半数美国人贫困或低薪\n【定位】Taking into account economic need beyond that absolute measure, the Institute for Policy\nStudies found that 140 million people are poor or low-income. That's almost half the\nU.S. population.\n考虑到绝对指标之外的经济需求，政策研究所发现有1.4亿人贫困或低收入。那几乎占美国\n人口的一半。\n【改写】nearly 50% of Americans ↔ almost half the U.S. population\nnearly 50%等于almost half\nare poor or receive low pay ↔ are poor or low-income\nreceive low pay等于low-income\n【辨邻】 M 71 percent、22 percent、37 percent描述的是对援助/福利支出态度的受访比例，而\n非'穷或低收入的人占总人口比例'，对象与题干不符。\n71 percent of respondents believe the country is spending too little on \"assistance to the poor\"", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Americans usually overestimate the number of blacks receiving welfare benefits.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】高估黑人福利受益者数量\n【定位】For example, Americans routinely overestimate the share of black recipients of public\nassistance programs.\n例如，美国人经常高估公共援助项目中黑人受益者的比例。\n【改写】overestimate the number of blacks receiving welfare benefits ↔\noverestimate the share of black recipients of public assistance programs\nnumber约等于share, welfare benefits等于public assistance programs\nusually ↔ routinely\nusually等于routinely", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "It is impossible for Americans to lift themselves out of poverty entirely on their own.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】无法独自摆脱贫困\n【定位】The big American myth is that you can pull yourself up by your own efforts and change a bad\nsituation into a good one. The reality is that finding opportunity without help from families,\nfriends, schools, and community is virtually impossible.\n美国的一个大神话是：你可以靠自己的努力把自己拉上去，把坏境况变成好境况。现实是：\n如果没有家人、朋友、学校和社区的帮助，几乎不可能找到机会。\n【改写】It is impossible for Americans to lift themselves out of poverty entirely on their own ↔\nfinding opportunity without help from families, friends, schools, and community is virtually\nimpossible\nlift themselves等于pull yourself up\n【辨邻】 O 该段说大多数人会经历经济困难、dip in and out of poverty，描述的是贫困流动现\n象，并未陈述'靠自己能否脱贫'这一论断。\nIndeed, people tend to dip in and out of poverty, perhaps due to unexpected obstacles like losing\na job", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Nowadays, it seems none of us can get away from income inequality.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】没人能逃脱收入不平等\n【定位】Today's faces of income inequality and lack of opportunity look like all of us.\n今天收入不平等和机会缺失的面孔，长得就像我们所有人。\n【改写】none of us can get away from income inequality ↔\nToday's faces of income inequality and lack of opportunity look like all of us\n正反同义\nNowadays ↔ Today's\n时间副词同义\n【辨邻】 E E段poverty touches virtually all of us和the one we each see in the mirror承接C\n段'all of us'的论断进一步铺陈，但本话题首次明确提出是在C段开头一句，E段仅是\n延展。\npoverty touches virtually all of us. The face of income inequality, for all but a very few of us, is the\none we each see in the mirror.\nP the issue of poverty touches all of us与陈述句意思接近，但P段是结尾的总结呼\n吁，话题首次明确陈述是在C段。\nto understand deeply that the issue of poverty touches all of us", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Assumptions about poor people become even more negative when they live on welfare.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】靠福利时成见更负面\n【定位】Assumptions get worse when people who are poor use government benefits to help\nthem survive.\n当贫困者依靠政府福利来维生时，对他们的种种假设会变得更糟。\n【改写】Assumptions about poor people become even more negative ↔ Assumptions get worse\nbecome more negative等于get worse\nwhen they live on welfare ↔\nwhen people who are poor use government benefits to help them survive\nlive on welfare等于use government benefits to survive\n【辨邻】 J J段说people in poverty are lazy, they don't care, and they haven't made the\nright decisions，是基础的负面假设，但并未陈述'靠福利时这些假设变得更糟'这一增\n量条件。\nlead us to the perception that people in poverty are lazy, they don't care, and they haven't made the\nright decisions", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "When are people likely to experience boredom, according to an accepted psychological definition?", "options": ["A) When they don't have the chance to do what they want.", "B) When they don't enjoy the materials they are studying.", "C) When they experience something unpleasant.", "D) When they engage in some routine activities."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】When are people likely to experience boredom, according to an accepted\npsychological definition?是 When 引导的事实型疑问句，且明确以according to an\naccepted psychological definition限定信息来源，答案应是原文定义句的客观转述。\n【拆句】题干主干是 When are people likely to experience boredom，主句问的是「时间/情\n境」；后置介词短语according to an accepted psychological definition为状语，限定答\n案来源必须是「公认的心理学定义」，不是作者本人观点或常识。\n【定位】One widely accepted psychological definition of boredom is \"the distasteful experience of\nwanting, but being unable, to engage in satisfying activity.\"\n无聊在心理学上一个被广为接受的定义是「想去从事一项令人满足的活动，却无法去做时的\n那种不快体验」。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nwanting, but being unable, to engage in satisfying activity ↔\ndon't have the chance to do what they want\nWhen they don't have the chance to do what they want.把原文定义句的两个核心成\n分「有意愿」与「被阻止」一一保留，且未添加任何原文未给出的限定，是定义句的最\n忠实改写。\nB) 无中生有\nWhen they don't enjoy the materials they are studying.把场景限定到「学习材\n料」，但原文定义未提学习场景，纯属添油加醋。\nC) 偷换概念\nWhen they experience something unpleasant.把原文里的「不快体验」拿出来当条\n件，可原文 distasteful experience 描述的是无聊本身（结果），不是触发无聊的条\n件，因果倒置。\nD) 与原文相反\nWhen they engage in some routine activities.把无聊触发条件描述为「在做日常\n活动」，可原文定义明确强调「想做却做不成」，主体是「未能从事」而非「正在\n从事」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the author say boredom can lead to?", "options": ["A) Determination.", "B) Concentration.", "C) Mental deterioration.", "D) Harmful conduct."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say boredom can lead to?是 What 引导的事实问句，问的是「无\n聊会导致什么」，是对原文具体陈述的转述。\n【拆句】句子主干 What ... boredom can lead to，是宾语前置的特殊疑问句；what 是 lead to 的\n宾语，问的是「后果」。句中嵌入does the author say并非考点，仅说明信息来源是作者\n陈述，结构无陷阱。\n【定位】Boredom has been linked to behavior issues including inattentive driving, mindless snacking,\nexcessive drinking, and addictive gambling.\n无聊与多种行为问题相关，包括驾驶时心不在焉、不动脑子地乱吃东西、过量饮酒以及嗜赌\n成瘾。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nDetermination.意为「决心」，原文从未提到无聊会让人变得有决心；整段讨论的全是\n负面后果，与正向心理品质毫无关联。\nB) 与原文相反\nConcentration.意为「专注」，与原文inattentive driving描述的「分心驾驶」恰好相\n反；无聊导致的是注意力涣散，不是专注。\nC) 过度推断\nMental deterioration.意为「精神/智力衰退」，是把原文里的具体行为问题进一步推到\n「精神层面退化」的程度，原文未提任何精神疾病或认知衰退后果。\nD) ✓ 正确\nbehavior issues including inattentive driving, mindless snacking, excessive drinking, and\naddictive gambling\n↔ Harmful conduct.\nHarmful conduct.既保留了 behavior=conduct 的词汇对应，又用 harmful 准确概括\n了那四个具体后果的共同属性，是对原文罗列最贴切的总括式改写。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What is the finding of one team of psychologists in their experiment?", "options": ["A) Volunteers prefer watching a boring movie to sitting alone deliberating.", "B) Many volunteers choose to hurt themselves rather than endure boredom.", "C) Male volunteers are more immune to the effects of boredom than females.", "D) Many volunteers are unable to resist boredom longer than fifteen minutes."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is the finding of one team of psychologists in their experiment?问的是具体实验\n的结论事实，需回到该实验描述句做同义比对，属典型细节题。\n【拆句】主干 What is the finding，主系表结构；of one team of psychologists 修饰 finding，in\ntheir experiment 进一步限定研究情境。三层修饰只是把范围限到「那个团队那次实验的结\n论」，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】One team of psychologists discovered that two-thirds of men and a quarter of women would\nrather self-administer electric shocks than sit alone with their thoughts for 15 minutes.\n一组心理学家发现，三分之二的男性与四分之一的女性宁愿对自己施加电击，也不愿独自静\n坐沉思 15 分钟。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nVolunteers prefer watching a boring movie to sitting alone deliberating.把后文另\n一项实验「看无聊片时电击自己」（another team asked volunteers to watch\nboring, sad, or neutral films）的实验对象混进来——这是另一团队的研究，对应另一\n题的结论，不是本题考的实验。\nB) ✓ 正确\nwould rather self-administer electric shocks than sit alone with their thoughts for 15 minutes ↔\nMany volunteers choose to hurt themselves rather than endure boredom.\nMany volunteers choose to hurt themselves rather than endure boredom.既保留\n了原文比较结构两端的方向，又把具体动作恰当上位化，是对实验发现的最忠实概括。\nC) 与原文相反\nMale volunteers are more immune to the effects of boredom than females.把男女\n比例的差异解读成「男性更能抗无聊」，可原文是「三分之二男性 vs 四分之一女性都选\n择了电击」——男性选择电击的比例反而更高，意味着男性更难忍无聊，结论方向完全\n相反。\nD) 过度推断\nMany volunteers are unable to resist boredom longer than fifteen minutes.把 15\n分钟当成志愿者能忍耐的上限，可原文只是说「实验设定独坐 15 分钟」，并未测出志愿\n者的耐受阈值——结论从实验时长推到「忍耐极限」，多迈了一步。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "Why does the author say boredom isn't all bad?", "options": ["A) It stimulates memorization.", "B) It allows time for relaxation.", "C) It may promote creative thinking.", "D) It may facilitate independent learning."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】Why does the author say boredom isn't all bad?问作者给出的理由，原文转折句后必紧\n跟解释，答案是该解释句的转述。\n【拆句】句首 Why 引出原因；主谓 does the author say 套着宾语从句 boredom isn't all bad。从\n句用 isn't all bad（部分否定）而非 isn't bad，表示「并非完全是坏事」，暗示后面一定有\n正面意义的展开。\n【定位】But boredom isn't all bad. By encouraging self-reflection and daydreaming, it can\nspur creativity.\n但无聊也并非全是坏事。通过促进自省与遐想，它能激发创造力。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nIt stimulates memorization.说无聊会刺激记忆，但原文谈的是 self-reflection、\ndaydreaming、creativity，全程没有 memory/memorization 这一概念。\nB) 偷换概念\nIt allows time for relaxation.把无聊的好处概括为「有时间放松」，可原文明确说的是\n促进自省与遐想进而激发创造力，重点是「创造产出」而非「身心放松」。\nC) ✓ 正确\nit can spur creativity ↔ It may promote creative thinking.\nIt may promote creative thinking.两端的动词（spur→promote）和名词\n（creativity→creative thinking）都做了精准的同义替换，且未引入任何超出原文的限\n定，是理由句的最忠实改写。\nD) 过度推断\nIt may facilitate independent learning.把好处推到「促进自主学习」，但原文谈的是\n创造力，而非学习或独立学习；从「自我反思」推到「独立学习」多走了一步。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author suggest one do when faced with a challenging problem?", "options": ["A) Stop idling and think big.", "B) Unlock one's smartphone.", "C) Look around oneself for stimulation.", "D) Allow oneself some time to be bored."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author suggest one do when faced with a challenging problem?问的\n是作者给出的具体行动建议，是原文建议句的客观转述，未要求推断。\n【拆句】主干 What does the author suggest one do，其中 one do 是 suggest 后省略 that 与\nshould 的虚拟语气从句；when faced with a challenging problem 是过去分词作时间状\n语，相当于 when one is faced with ...，意为「当面对难题时」。\n【定位】Watch paint dry or water boil, or at least put away your smartphone for a while, and you\nmight unlock your next big idea.\n看油漆变干，看水烧开，或者至少先把手机放下一会儿，你就可能解开下一个大点子。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nStop idling and think big.让人停止闲散、去想大事，可原文恰恰建议你 idle 一会儿\n（看油漆干、看水开就是 idling）——方向完全相反。\nB) 与原文相反\nUnlock one's smartphone.让人解锁手机，可原文建议的是put away your\nsmartphone，即放下手机；动作方向完全相反。\nC) 与原文相反\nLook around oneself for stimulation.让人四处寻找刺激，可原文建议恰好是减少刺激\n（看油漆、看水开都是极低刺激场景，放下手机是切断刺激源）——方向相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\nWatch paint dry or water boil, or at least put away your smartphone for a while ↔\nAllow oneself some time to be bored.\nAllow oneself some time to be bored.既呼应了尾段「主动放空」的具体动作，又与\n全文「无聊能激发创造力」的主旨完全对齐，是对建议句最贴切的上位概括。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What is catching environmentalists' attention nowadays?", "options": ["A) Rich countries are stripping poor ones of their resources.", "B) Forests are fast shrinking in many developing countries.", "C) Forests are eating away the fertile farmland worldwide.", "D) Rich countries are doing little to address deforestation."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is catching environmentalists' attention nowadays? 是直接的特殊疑问句，问的\n是一个具体事实，不含 infer、suggest、imply，也不问全篇主旨。\n【拆句】题干 What is catching environmentalists' attention nowadays? 是现在进行时的特殊疑\n问句，主语是疑问代词 What，谓语 is catching 带宾语 environmentalists' attention，时\n间状语 nowadays 在句末。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Forests in countries like Brazil and the Congo get a lot of attention from environmentalists,\nand it is easy to see why. South America and sub-Saharan Africa are experiencing\ndeforestation on an enormous scale: every year almost 5 million hectares are lost.\n巴西、刚果这些国家的森林正得到环保人士的大量关注，原因不难看出。南美和撒哈拉以南\n非洲正以巨大规模经历森林砍伐：每年几乎损失 500 万公顷。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文只指出环保人士关注发展中地区的森林消失，并没说 Rich countries are stripping\npoor ones of their resources，这是套用国际关系常见话术编造出的内容。\nB) ✓ 正确\nBrazil and the Congo ↔ many developing countries\nexperiencing deforestation on an enormous scale ↔ fast shrinking\n预判说「发展中地区森林大规模消失」，Forests are fast shrinking in many\ndeveloping countries. 在主语、动词、地域限定三处都与原文等值，是定位句的同义\n替换。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文讲的是森林正在消失，选项却说 Forests are eating away the fertile farmland\nworldwide——森林在侵蚀农田，主客体关系恰好颠倒；首段也未提及 worldwide\nfarmland 被吞噬。\nD) 答非所问\n题干问的是 What is catching environmentalists' attention nowadays?——环保人士\n关注的是什么，而 Rich countries are doing little to address deforestation 是对富国\n不作为的指责，原文首段并没有这一指控。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "Which countries have the fastest forest growth?", "options": ["A) Those that have newly achieved independence.", "B) Those that have the greatest demand for timber.", "C) Those that used to have the lowest forest coverage.", "D) Those that provide enormous government subsidies."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】Which countries have the fastest forest growth? 直接问「哪些国家」，是对原文具体信\n息的客观提取。\n【拆句】题干 Which countries have the fastest forest growth? 是 Which 引导的特殊疑问句，\nWhich countries 作主语，have 作谓语，宾语 the fastest forest growth。结构简单。\n【定位】Forests are spreading in almost all Western countries, with the fastest growth in places that\nhistorically had rather few trees.\n几乎所有西方国家的森林都在扩展，其中增长最快的地方是那些历史上树木相当稀少的\n国家。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\n原文只用 Ireland 作为戏剧性案例（1922 年独立时 1%、现在 11%），并未说「森林增\n长最快的是新近独立的国家」这一普遍规律，Those that have newly achieved\nindependence. 是把单一举例拔高为通则。\nB) 无中生有\nthe greatest demand for timber 在 P2 全段从未出现，森林增长最快的原因被原文限\n定为「历史上树少」，而非木材需求。\nC) ✓ 正确\nhistorically had rather few trees ↔ used to have the lowest forest coverage\n预判说「历史上树木很少的国家」，Those that used to have the lowest forest\ncoverage. 把「过去树少」换说成「过去森林覆盖率最低」，二者完全同义，因此\n锁定。\nD) 张冠李戴\nenormous government subsidies 对应的是 P3 提到的 government policy and\nsubsidy，那讨论的是「促进森林增长的因素」，不是 Q52 所问的「哪国增长最快」，\n跨段错位。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What has encouraged forest growth historically?", "options": ["A) The government's advocacy.", "B) The use of wood for fuel.", "C) The favourable climate.", "D) The green movement."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What has encouraged forest growth historically? 问的是历史上具体促进森林增长的因\n素，是对文中事实的提取，不要求多步推理。\n【拆句】题干 What has encouraged forest growth historically? 是现在完成时特殊疑问句，What\n作主语，has encouraged 作谓语，forest growth 作宾语，historically 是时间状语。现在\n完成时强调「从过去延续到现在」的累积效应。\n【定位】Throughout history, governments have protected and promoted forests for diverse reasons,\nranging from the need for wooden warships to a desire to promote suburban house-building.\n纵观历史，政府出于各种理由保护并推动森林的扩张，从制造木质战舰的需要，到希望促进\n郊区住宅建设。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ngovernments have protected and promoted forests ↔ The government's advocacy\n预判说「历史上是政府保护并推动了森林」，The government's advocacy. 用名词形式\n精准对应原文动词短语 promoted。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文虽提到 the need for wooden warships，但 warships 是用木材造战舰，并非烧木\n头做燃料；The use of wood for fuel 偷换了用途。\nC) 无中生有\n原文讲历史上促进森林的是政府政策，没有提到 favourable climate 这一自然因素；这\n是套用常识编造。\nD) 张冠李戴\nThe green movement 属于近现代环保运动，对应原文 Nowadays 这一时间段，而题\n干限定的是 historically 历史范围，时段不匹配。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What accounts for our increasing desire for forests?", "options": ["A) Their unique scenic beauty.", "B) Their use as fruit plantations.", "C) Their capability of improving air quality.", "D) Their stable supply of building materials."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What accounts for our increasing desire for forests? 用 account for 直接问原因，对应\n文中应有明确的因果句，属于事实信息提取。\n【拆句】题干 What accounts for our increasing desire for forests? 中 account for 是固定短语，\n意为「解释、是…的原因」。基础薄弱考生易把它误读成「算作」，须当心；除此之外句式\n简单。\n【定位】Nowadays forests are increasingly welcome because they suck in carbon pollution from\nthe air.\n如今森林越来越受欢迎，因为它们能吸走空气中的碳污染。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nunique scenic beauty 在 P3 中并不存在；末段反而说 Others simply dislike the\nappearance of forests planted in neat rows，强调有人对景观不满，把「景观美」作\n为渴求理由完全是无中生有。\nB) 无中生有\nuse as fruit plantations 在 P3 全段未出现，原文用 plantations 是泛指「种植园」，\n与水果无关。\nC) ✓ 正确\nsuck in carbon pollution from the air ↔ capability of improving air quality\n预判说「森林能净化空气」，Their capability of improving air quality. 是这一句的标\n准学术化转述，因此锁定。\nD) 张冠李戴\nsupply of building materials 对应原文 wooden warships 与 suburban house-\nbuilding，但那都是 Throughout history 段提及的历史理由，不是 Q54 所问的「如今\n越发渴求」的理由。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author conclude about the prospects of forestation?", "options": ["A) Deserts in sub-Saharan Africa will diminish gradually.", "B) It will play a more and more important role in people's lives.", "C) Forest destruction in the developing world will quickly slow down.", "D) Developed and developing countries are moving in opposite directions."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题）\n【判型】What does the author conclude about the prospects of forestation? 问的是作者对整\n体前景的结论，覆盖全文走向，不可由单一定位句直接拿到。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author conclude about the prospects of forestation? 是宾语从句\n结构的特殊疑问句，What 是 conclude 的宾语，about the prospects of forestation 是\nconclude 的状语，限定结论的话题对象。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nDeserts in sub-Saharan Africa will diminish gradually. 蹭了 sub-Saharan Africa 这\n一地名，但话题对象从「森林」被偷换为「沙漠」，原文从未讨论沙漠消长。\nB) 过度推断\nIt will play a more and more important role in people's lives. 是抒情式价值判断，\n与原文末句「两条趋势同样不可阻挡」的冷静对照不匹配；原文从未把森林作用提升到\n「在人们生活中越发重要」的层面。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文末句明说 The growth of Western forests seems almost as unstoppable as\ndeforestation elsewhere——他处毁林同样不可阻挡，而 Forest destruction in the\ndeveloping world will quickly slow down. 却说「很快放缓」，方向相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\nThe growth of Western forests seems almost as unstoppable as deforestation elsewhere ↔\nmoving in opposite directions\nBut forests are also changing in rich Western countries. They are growing larger ↔ Developed\nSouth America and sub-Saharan Africa ↔ developing\nDeveloped and developing countries are moving in opposite directions. 同时容纳\n了全文「造林与毁林」两条主线，与末句并置结构完美对齐，是唯一覆盖全文且不与任\n何区间冲突的选项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}]});
